import { nativeOlympiadContent, p, h, sub, fig } from "./native-content";

// Source PDFs and crop provenance: docs/engineering/native-archive-batch-8-*.json.

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2008-t1"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Water-powered rice-pounding mortar"),
    p("A lever with a pestle and a hollow water bucket pivots about a horizontal axis T. When horizontal, the pestle rests on rice in the mortar. The dry lever and pestle have mass \\(M=30\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\), inertia \\(I_L=12\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2}\\), and centre G with TG horizontal in the horizontal configuration. Water enters at mass rate Φ. Use water density \\(\\rho=1000\\,\\mathrm{kg/m^3}\\), \\(g=9.81\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}\\), and the bucket geometry in the diagram: \\(L=0.74\\,\\mathrm m\\), \\(a=0.20\\,\\mathrm m\\), \\(b=0.15\\,\\mathrm m\\), \\(h=0.12\\,\\mathrm m\\), sloping end angle \\(30^\\circ\\). The lever thickness is 8 cm. Neglect pivot friction, incoming-water impact and, when specified, water flow during motion. Assume water surfaces remain horizontal. Tilt α increases as the water bucket goes down and the pestle rises. During a working cycle water fills at α=0, the lever tips, water spills from α1 until empty at α2, inertia carries it to α0, then the empty lever falls back and pounds the rice. At β the spilling bucket counterbalances the dry lever. A sufficiently large inflow can sustain an elevated rest mode instead of pounding."),
    fig("batch8-mortar-geometry", "Original rice mortar dimensions, bucket profile, pivot T and dry mass centre G."),
    fig("batch8-mortar-cycle", "Original six phases of filling, lifting, spilling, empty turning and pounding."),
    sub("1.1. Dry centre position"),
    p("The lever first lifts when the bucket contains 1.0 kg of water. Find TG."),
    sub("1.2. Spilling and empty angles"),
    p("Find α1, when the initial 1.0 kg just begins to spill, and α2, when the bucket empties."),
    sub("1.3. Balanced spilling bucket"),
    p("Find β and the remaining water mass m1 when total gravitational torque vanishes."),
    sub("2.1. Torque cycle"),
    p("For very small constant inflow, neglect water added while moving and neglect changes of inertia. Sketch total torque μ against α over a complete cycle, explicitly labelling α=0, α1 and α2. Choose positive torque to increase α."),
    sub("2.2. Work from the torque graph"),
    p("Explain geometrically the total work done by the gravitational torque over the cycle and the energy delivered to the rice."),
    sub("2.3. Maximum tilt and pounding estimate"),
    p("Estimate α0 and Wpound from a piecewise-linear approximation to the torque graph. State the approximations."),
    sub("3.1.1. Elevated equilibrium stability"),
    p("Now retain inflow during motion and suppose the bucket always remains full to its spill edge. Sketch torque near β and identify the equilibrium."),
    sub("3.1.2. Small-angle restoring torque"),
    p("Find the first-order torque near α=β+Δα. Use the source's approximation that the water moment arm can be fixed at its equilibrium value."),
    sub("3.1.3. Small-oscillation period"),
    p("The lever is released from β+Δα with zero angular velocity. Derive the approximate harmonic motion and calculate its period, retaining the water's inertia."),
    sub("3.2. Inflow for one-degree oscillations"),
    p("Find the minimum mass inflow Φ1 that can keep the bucket overflowing during a one-degree harmonic oscillation about β."),
    sub("3.3. Inflow that prevents pounding"),
    p("Assume the lever motion can be estimated harmonically. Find the minimum inflow Φ2 that sustains overflow as the angle falls to α1 and prevents operation."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Water-powered rice-pounding mortar"),
    sub("1.1. Dry centre position"),
    p("The horizontal bucket-bottom length is \\(d=L-h\\cot30^\\circ=0.5322\\,\\mathrm m\\). With V=0.001 m³ the shallow-water depth c obeys \\(V=bcd+bc^2\\cot30^\\circ/2\\), giving c=0.01228 m. To the source's shallow-layer accuracy, the horizontal water moment arm is \\(TH\\simeq a+d/2+c\\cot30^\\circ/4=0.4714\\,\\mathrm m\\). Balance \\(M\\,TG=m\\,TH\\), giving \\(TG=0.0157\\,\\mathrm m\\simeq0.016\\,\\mathrm m\\)."),
    sub("1.2. Spilling and empty angles"),
    p("At first spill the triangular water section has bottom length \\(x=2V/(bh)=0.1111\\,\\mathrm m\\). Geometry gives \\(\\tan\\alpha_1=h/(x+h\\cot30^\\circ)\\), hence \\(\\alpha_1=20.6^\\circ\\). The bucket is empty at \\(\\alpha_2=30.0^\\circ\\)."),
    sub("1.3. Balanced spilling bucket"),
    p("For a spilling triangular section, \\(m=\\rho bhx/2=9x\\) in SI units. Its moment arm along the lever is \\(TN=L+a-2h\\cot30^\\circ/3-x/3=0.8014-x/3\\). Balance \\(mTN=M\\,TG=0.4714\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m}\\), giving \\(-3x^2+7.213x=0.4714\\). Choose the geometric root x=0.0672 m, not the root larger than the bucket. Then m1≈0.605 kg and \\(\\beta=\\arctan[h/(x+h\\cot30^\\circ)]=23.6^\\circ\\). The source accidentally prints TG=0.1571 m in one substitution, although its arithmetic and final result use 0.01571 m."),
    sub("2.1. Torque cycle"),
    p("At α=0 the torque rises during filling from −4.62 N m to zero. On the outward constant-mass branch it rises to μ(α1)=+2.69 N m, then falls through μ(β)=0 to μ(α2)=−4.00 N m. Beyond α2, and throughout empty return, \\(\\mu=-Mg\\,TG\\cos\\alpha=-4.62\\cos\\alpha\\,\\mathrm{N\\,m}\\). Thus the empty-return endpoint is μ(0)=−4.62 N m, different from the just-lifting endpoint at the same angle. Draw the path with arrows rather than a single-valued graph at α=0."),
    sub("2.2. Work from the torque graph"),
    p("Infinitesimal work is \\(dW=\\mu\\,d\\alpha\\), with α in radians. The signed enclosed area is \\(W_{total}=\\oint\\mu\\,d\\alpha\\). The pestle's impact energy is the kinetic energy acquired on empty return: \\[W_{pound}=Mg\\,TG\\sin\\alpha_0.\\] This is the area between the empty-return torque curve and the α axis from 0 to α0. In the stipulated negligible-water-kinetic-energy model, the closed-cycle work supplies the impact energy."),
    sub("2.3. Maximum tilt and pounding estimate"),
    p("Equate the positive outward area to the negative outward area at the turning point. A triangle of height 2.7 N m from 0 to β and a trapezoid falling to about −4 N m give \\(23.6(2.7)/2\\simeq4[(\\alpha_0-23.6)+(\\alpha_0-30)]/2\\). The common degree-to-radian factor cancels only in this area equality. Thus α0≈34.7° and \\(W_{pound}=4.62\\sin34.7^\\circ\\simeq2.6\\,\\mathrm J\\). These are graph-based estimates, not exact quadrature."),
    sub("3.1.1. Elevated equilibrium stability"),
    p("The filling and draining branches coincide when the bucket always spills. Torque decreases through zero as α increases through β, so a positive displacement has negative torque and vice versa. The equilibrium is stable."),
    sub("3.1.2. Small-angle restoring torque"),
    p("The overflowing water mass is \\(m(\\alpha)=\\rho bh^2(\\cot\\alpha-\\cot30^\\circ)/2\\), so \\(\\Delta m=-\\rho bh^2\\Delta\\alpha/(2\\sin^2\\beta)\\). With TN≈M TG/m1≈0.779 m fixed, \\[\\mu\\simeq-\\frac{\\rho bh^2g\\,TN\\cos\\beta}{2\\sin^2\\beta}\\Delta\\alpha\\simeq-47.2\\Delta\\alpha\\,\\mathrm{N\\,m}.\\] Δα is in radians. Including the smaller change of the centroid arm modifies this approximate coefficient by a few percent."),
    sub("3.1.3. Small-oscillation period"),
    p("Treat the equilibrium water as a point mass: \\(I_{eff}\\simeq I_L+m_1TN^2\\simeq12.4\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2}\\). The linear equation is \\(I_{eff}\\ddot{\\Delta\\alpha}+47.2\\Delta\\alpha=0\\). Hence \\(\\Delta\\alpha(t)=\\Delta\\alpha(0)\\cos(2\\pi t/\\tau)\\), with \\(\\tau=2\\pi\\sqrt{I_{eff}/47.2}\\simeq3.2\\,\\mathrm s\\)."),
    sub("3.2. Inflow for one-degree oscillations"),
    p("The greatest refill rate occurs as the angle decreases fastest through β. For angular amplitude A in radians, \\[\\Phi_{min}=\\frac{\\pi\\rho bh^2A}{\\tau\\sin^2\\beta}.\\] With A=π/180 and τ≈3.2 s, Φ1≈0.23 kg/s."),
    sub("3.3. Inflow that prevents pounding"),
    p("The limiting amplitude is \\(\\beta-\\alpha_1\\simeq3.0^\\circ\\). The required flow is proportional to amplitude, so \\(\\Phi_2\\simeq3\\Phi_1\\simeq0.7\\,\\mathrm{kg/s}\\). Above it the return can remain on the elevated oscillatory branch instead of striking the rice. This is the source's harmonic estimate."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2021-t2"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Electrostatic lens"),
    p("A metallic ring of radius R carries uniform charge q. Its circular toroidal cross-section has radius a≪R, neglected except in part D. Its plane is xy and its axis is z. Potential is zero at infinity. An electron has mass m and charge −e, e>0, and does not perturb the ring charge distribution. Near the origin use \\((1+x)^s\\simeq1+sx+s(s-1)x^2/2\\). In lens operation the electron speed v along z is approximately constant, with kinetic energy E=mv²/2. The ring is activated only while the electron packet crosses \\(|z|<d/2\\), where d≪R. Work in the paraxial limit r≪R, with focal distances much greater than d, ignoring magnetic fields and propagation delay. Parts D and E replace instantaneous charging by a capacitance C and contact resistance R0. For focusing choose the appropriate signed supply V0."),
    fig("batch8-lens-ring", "Original charged ring, axial coordinate z, major radius R and toroidal cross section 2a."),
    sub("a.1. Axial potential"),
    p("Find Φ(z) on the axis."),
    sub("a.2. Near-centre axial expansion"),
    p("Expand through the first position-dependent nonzero term for |z|≪R."),
    sub("a.3. Axial electron force"),
    p("Find the leading signed axial force and the sign of q needed for oscillations."),
    sub("a.4. Axial oscillation frequency"),
    p("For the stable sign of q find ω."),
    sub("b.1. Radial potential coefficient"),
    p("In the ring plane let Φ(r)≈q(α+βr²). Derive β."),
    sub("b.2. Radial electron force"),
    p("Find the leading radial force and the sign of q for radial harmonic confinement."),
    sub("c.1. Pulsed lens focal length"),
    fig("batch8-lens-pulse", "Original electron packet passing a thin active region and converging at a focus."),
    p("With instantaneous switching, find f in terms of β and the given quantities. Initially the electron packet is parallel to z."),
    sub("c.2. Finite-source image"),
    p("A point source is on the axis distance b>f from the ring and emits at small angles. Find image distance c."),
    sub("c.3. Thin-lens relation"),
    p("Calculate 1/b+1/c and determine whether the optical thin-lens relation holds."),
    sub("d.1. Thin torus capacitance"),
    p("Retain cross-section radius a≪R and calculate C to leading thin-ring accuracy."),
    sub("d.2. Charging waveform"),
    fig("batch8-lens-circuit", "Original switch connecting the ring capacitance through contact resistance to voltage V0 or ground."),
    p("At entry connect the ring through R0 to V0, and at exit connect it through R0 to ground. Time t=0 is passage through the ring plane. Find and sketch q(t), and give its maximum signed charge q0."),
    sub("e.1. Focal length with finite charging"),
    p("Assume f/v≫R0C and d/v is comparable to R0C. Find f, including the discharge tail after the electron exits the nominal active region."),
    sub("e.2. Effective charge"),
    p("Express qeff such that the result has the instantaneous-lens form with q replaced by qeff."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Electrostatic lens"),
    sub("a.1. Axial potential"),
    p("Every ring element is the same distance \\(\\sqrt{R^2+z^2}\\) away, so \\[\\Phi(z)=\\frac q{4\\pi\\epsilon_0\\sqrt{R^2+z^2}}.\\]"),
    sub("a.2. Near-centre axial expansion"),
    p("Binomial expansion gives \\[\\Phi(z)\\simeq\\frac q{4\\pi\\epsilon_0R}\\left(1-\\frac{z^2}{2R^2}\\right).\\]"),
    sub("a.3. Axial electron force"),
    p("Because electron potential energy is −eΦ, \\(F_z=e\\partial_z\\Phi=-eqz/(4\\pi\\epsilon_0R^3)\\). It restores toward the centre for q>0."),
    sub("a.4. Axial oscillation frequency"),
    p("Compare \\(m\\ddot z=-m\\omega^2z\\) with A3: \\(\\omega=\\sqrt{eq/(4\\pi\\epsilon_0mR^3)}\\), with q>0."),
    sub("b.1. Radial potential coefficient"),
    p("Expand \\((R^2+r^2-2Rr\\cos\\phi)^{-1/2}\\) and average cosφ and cos²φ around the ring. The result is \\(\\Phi(r)=q[1+r^2/(4R^2)]/(4\\pi\\epsilon_0R)\\). Thus \\(\\beta=1/(16\\pi\\epsilon_0R^3)\\). This also follows from the charge-free Laplace equation and the axial expansion."),
    sub("b.2. Radial electron force"),
    p("\\(F_r=e\\partial_r\\Phi=2eq\\beta r=eqr/(8\\pi\\epsilon_0R^3)\\). Radial restoration requires q<0, opposite to axial restoration. The ring cannot provide simultaneous static confinement in all directions."),
    sub("c.1. Pulsed lens focal length"),
    p("The active time is d/v. Radial impulse gives \\(\\Delta v_r=2eq\\beta rd/(mv)\\), negative for q<0. The subsequent crossing time is −r/Δvr. Thus \\[f=-\\frac{mv^2}{2eq\\beta d}=-\\frac E{eq\\beta d}.\\]"),
    sub("c.2. Finite-source image"),
    p("At the ring the initial radial velocity is vr/b. Add the impulse from C1, then propagate to the axis: \\[c=-\\left(\\frac1b+\\frac{eq\\beta d}{E}\\right)^{-1}.\\] With b>f and q<0 the denominator is negative and c>0."),
    sub("c.3. Thin-lens relation"),
    p("C2 gives \\(1/b+1/c=-eq\\beta d/E=1/f\\). Thus the same thin-lens equation holds in the stated impulse approximation."),
    sub("d.1. Thin torus capacitance"),
    p("Split the ring at a small angular cutoff η with a/R≪η≪1. The nearby piece is approximately a straight line and contributes \\(q\\ln(2\\eta R/a)/(4\\pi^2\\epsilon_0R)\\). The remainder contributes \\(q\\ln(4/\\eta)/(4\\pi^2\\epsilon_0R)\\). The arbitrary cutoff cancels, giving \\[C=\\frac{4\\pi^2\\epsilon_0R}{\\ln(8R/a)}.\\]"),
    sub("d.2. Charging waveform"),
    p("Write τ=R0C and t0=d/v. The continuous waveform is \\[q(t)=\\begin{cases}0&t<-t_0/2,\\\\CV_0[1-e^{-(t+t_0/2)/\\tau}]&-t_0/2\\le t\\le t_0/2,\\\\CV_0(1-e^{-t_0/\\tau})e^{-(t-t_0/2)/\\tau}&t>t_0/2.\\end{cases}\\] The largest magnitude occurs at exit, with \\(q_0=CV_0(1-e^{-d/(vR_0C)})\\). For focusing V0<0, so the curve dips below zero and returns exponentially to it."),
    sub("e.1. Focal length with finite charging"),
    p("During the impulse r changes negligibly and the electron remains near the ring because vR0C is of order d≪R. Integrating \\(R_0\\dot q+q/C=V(t)\\) over the full charging and discharge history gives \\(\\int qdt=CV_0d/v\\), independent of R0. Therefore \\(\\Delta v_r=(2e\\beta r/m)CV_0d/v\\) and \\[f=-\\frac E{e\\beta CV_0d}.\\] Omitting the discharge tail would give a different and incomplete impulse."),
    sub("e.2. Effective charge"),
    p("The complete charge-time integral matches a rectangular pulse of height \\(q_{eff}=CV_0\\) and duration d/v. It is not the peak charge q0."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2023-t1"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Characterization of soil colloids"),
    p("A one-dimensional colloidal particle obeys \\(M\\dot v=-\\gamma v+F(t)+F_{ext}(t)\\). Random molecular collisions supply impulses. In A, Fext=0. In B, approximate its velocity by independent constants vn on intervals \\((n-1)\\delta<t\\le n\\delta\\), with zero mean and \\(\\langle v_nv_m\\rangle=C\\delta_{nm}\\). Initially x=v=0. For C a dilute suspension of positive charge Q flows in a narrow channel of cross-section A. Neglect particle interactions, walls, background ions and gravity. A field E points along x. The diffusion flux is \\(J_D=-D\\,dn/dx\\), the drift flux is JQ=nu, and osmotic pressure is Π=nkT with k=R/NA. For D a spherical particle has a=5.0 μm in water of viscosity η=\\(8.9\\times10^{-4}\\) Pa s at T=25°C. Use γ=6πaη and R=8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. Required experimental plots are reproduced below."),
    sub("a.1. Single impulse"),
    p("A collision at t0 supplies impulse I0 to a resting particle. Determine v0 and τ in \\(v(t)=v_0e^{-(t-t_0)/\\tau}\\)."),
    sub("a.2. Successive collisions"),
    p("Impulses Ii occur at ti after t=0. Find v(t) and specify which collision times contribute."),
    sub("b.1. Displacement moments"),
    p("At t=Nδ find the mean displacement and its mean square in terms of C, δ and t."),
    sub("b.2. Continuum scaling"),
    p("For a finite nonzero Brownian mean square as δ tends to zero, find α and β in C∝δα and MSD∝tβ."),
    sub("c.1. Right-going crossing rate"),
    fig("batch8-colloid-channel", "Original electrophoresis channel, concentration profile and crossing region."),
    p("Half the particles have speed +v and half −v. Linearize n(x) about x0. Find N+(x0), the right-going number crossing per area per time, using n0=n(x0) and s=dn/dx at x0."),
    sub("c.2. Diffusion coefficient"),
    p("Use the analogous N− and ⟨v²⟩=C to find JD, D and the MSD."),
    sub("c.3. Osmotic balance"),
    p("For the stationary concentration under E, balance osmotic forces on a slice of width Δx. Find dn/dx in terms of n, Q, E, k and T."),
    sub("c.4. Drift velocity"),
    p("With Fext=QE, zero mean initial velocity and ⟨F⟩=0, find ⟨v(t)⟩ and terminal velocity u."),
    sub("c.5. Einstein relation"),
    p("Use JD+JQ=0 to express D in terms of k, T and γ."),
    sub("d.1. Experimental Avogadro estimate"),
    fig("batch8-colloid-histogram", "Original measured displacement histogram and complete frequency table in micrometres."),
    p("Using the histogram of displacements sampled at Δt=60 s, estimate NA to two significant digits. Do not use a known value of k or NA."),
    sub("d.2. Drift crossover"),
    p("Replace each vn by u+vn. Find the MSD, its small- and large-time power laws, the crossover t*, and sketch a log-log plot."),
    sub("d.3. Swimming microbes"),
    fig("batch8-microbe-msd", "Original logarithmic mean-square-displacement plot with three asymptotic slopes."),
    p("A microbe swims at randomly selected velocity ±u0 for independent intervals δ0, in addition to Brownian motion. The supplied MSD plot has three regimes. Express each power law using D, u0, δ0 and t."),
    sub("e.1. Coagulation threshold"),
    p("Two colloidal particles interact through \\(U(d)=-A/d+B\\epsilon(kT)^2e^{-d/\\lambda}/q^2\\), with A,B>0, dielectric constant ε, ion charges ±q, and \\(\\lambda=\\sqrt{\\epsilon kT/(2N_Aq^2c)}\\). Neglect thermal fluctuations and assume instantaneous terminal motion. Find the lowest salt concentration c that guarantees coagulation."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Characterization of soil colloids"),
    sub("a.1. Single impulse"),
    p("Integrate through the collision to obtain Mv0=I0. Afterwards Mdv/dt=−γv. Thus \\(v_0=I_0/M\\) and \\(\\tau=M/\\gamma\\). The field checks τ."),
    sub("a.2. Successive collisions"),
    p("Linearity permits superposition: \\[v(t)=\\sum_{0<t_i<t}\\frac{I_i}{M}e^{-(t-t_i)/\\tau}.\\] At an impulse include the instantaneous velocity jump Ii/M if using a right-continuous convention."),
    sub("b.1. Displacement moments"),
    p("Write Δx=δΣvn. Each mean vanishes and every cross correlation vanishes. Therefore \\(\\langle\\Delta x\\rangle=0\\) and \\(\\langle\\Delta x^2\\rangle=N C\\delta^2=C\\delta t\\). The field checks the mean square."),
    sub("b.2. Continuum scaling"),
    p("The product Cδ must remain finite. Hence α=−1 and β=1."),
    sub("c.1. Right-going crossing rate"),
    p("Only particles between x0−vδ and x0 cross. Thus \\(N_+=\\frac1{2\\delta}\\int_{x_0-v\\delta}^{x_0}[n_0+s(x-x_0)]dx=n_0v/2-sv^2\\delta/4\\)."),
    sub("c.2. Diffusion coefficient"),
    p("\\(N_-=n_0v/2+sv^2\\delta/4\\), giving \\(J_D=-sC\\delta/2\\). Therefore \\(D=C\\delta/2\\) and \\(\\langle\\Delta x^2\\rangle=2Dt\\). The field checks D."),
    sub("c.3. Osmotic balance"),
    p("Force balance is Π(x)A+nAΔxQE=Π(x+Δx)A. Since Π=nkT, \\(dn/dx=nQE/(kT)\\)."),
    sub("c.4. Drift velocity"),
    p("The mean obeys \\(M d\\langle v\\rangle/dt=-\\gamma\\langle v\\rangle+QE\\). Thus \\(\\langle v\\rangle=(QE/\\gamma)(1-e^{-t/\\tau})\\) and \\(u=QE/\\gamma\\). The field checks u."),
    sub("c.5. Einstein relation"),
    p("Substitute C3 and u=QE/γ into −Ddn/dx+nu=0. Cancelling nQE gives \\(D=kT/\\gamma\\)."),
    sub("d.1. Experimental Avogadro estimate"),
    p("Take the frequency-weighted mean square of the histogram, approximately 6.34 μm². Since \\(\\langle\\Delta x^2\\rangle=RT\\Delta t/(3\\pi a\\eta N_A)\\), obtain \\(N_A\\simeq5.6\\times10^{23}\\,\\mathrm{mol^{-1}}\\). This is the estimate from these data, not the modern defined constant."),
    sub("d.2. Drift crossover"),
    p("The nonzero mean adds a squared drift term: \\(\\langle\\Delta x^2\\rangle=u^2t^2+2Dt\\). The terms match at \\(t_*=2D/u^2\\). The log-log slope rises from 1 at t≪t* to 2 at t≫t*. Draw continuous asymptotes meeting near t*."),
    sub("d.3. Swimming microbes"),
    p("If 2D/u0²≪δ0, the successive laws are \\(2Dt\\) for t≪2D/u0², \\(u_0^2t^2\\) for 2D/u0²≪t≪δ0, and \\(u_0^2\\delta_0t\\) for t≫δ0. More exactly the last coefficient is u0²δ0+2D. The assumed intermediate regime makes its Brownian contribution negligible. The source solution labels this item D.1 in its prose, but its answer box and question identify D.3."),
    sub("e.1. Coagulation threshold"),
    p("Under overdamped deterministic motion every repulsive-force interval must disappear. The threshold satisfies U′=U″=0, giving d=2λ and \\(\\lambda=e^2Aq^2/[4B\\epsilon(kT)^2]\\), where e is Euler's number. Hence \\[c=\\frac{8B^2\\epsilon^3(kT)^5}{e^4N_AA^2q^6}.\\] Setting only the potential barrier height to zero would not eliminate the force barrier and does not meet the stated deterministic criterion. The value above is the critical concentration, an infimum for finite-time coagulation from initially separated particles. At equality the force has a double zero at \\(d=2\\lambda\\), approached only asymptotically in this overdamped model. Finite-time passage requires \\(c>c_{\\rm crit}\\)."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2024-t1"] = {
  statement: [
    h("The greenhouse effect"),
    p("Use Planck spectral exitance \\(u(\\lambda,T)=2\\pi hc^2/[\\lambda^5(e^{hc/(\\lambda k_BT)}-1)]\\), Stefan-Boltzmann exitance σT⁴ and Kirchhoff equality of absorptivity and emissivity at each wavelength. Take hc=1240 eV nm, kB=\\(8.62\\times10^{-5}\\) eV/K, σ=\\(5.67\\times10^{-8}\\) W m⁻² K⁻⁴. The blackbody Sun has TS=5770 K, RS=\\(6.96\\times10^{8}\\) m and distance d=\\(1.50\\times10^{11}\\) m. Earth is isothermal and in equilibrium. Give parametric results and numerical values to three significant figures. The solar constant S0 is solar power per area normal to the rays at Earth, integrated over wavelength. In B, a thin atmospheric layer has the same area as Earth. It reflects visible/UV fraction rA=0.255 from either direction, transmits the rest and absorbs none. Earth's surface reflects visible/UV fraction rE and absorbs all infrared. The atmosphere absorbs infrared fraction ε, transmits the rest and reflects none. Approximate each source spectrum by its dominant wavelength band."),
    sub("a-1. Solar constant"),
    p("Find S0."),
    sub("a-2. Airless blackbody Earth"),
    p("Neglect the atmosphere and find TE."),
    sub("a-3. Wien equation"),
    p("Write the equation f(x)=0 for the nonzero maximum, with x=hc/(λkBT)."),
    sub("a-4. Wien constant"),
    p("Calculate xm and b in λmaxT=b."),
    sub("a-5. Solar and terrestrial peaks"),
    p("Find λmax for the Sun and airless Earth."),
    sub("a-6. Equal-height rescaling"),
    fig("batch8-greenhouse-spectra", "Original equal-height scaled solar and terrestrial blackbody spectra."),
    p("Solar spectral irradiance at Earth is \\(\\tilde u_S=(R_S/d)^2u(\\lambda,T_S)\\). Find γ so that γũS and u(λ,TE) have equal peak heights."),
    sub("b-1. Opaque infrared atmosphere"),
    fig("batch8-greenhouse-flows", "Original shortwave transmission and reflection and infrared absorption and re-emission."),
    p("For ε=1 and rE=0, find TE and TA."),
    sub("b-2. Multiple-reflection albedo"),
    p("Find combined albedo α in terms of rE,rA. Evaluate at rE=0.102."),
    sub("b-3a. Partially absorbing atmosphere"),
    p("Find TE as a function of σ,α,S0,ε with no non-radiative heat flow."),
    sub("b-3b. Present-temperature emissivity"),
    p("Find ε giving TE=288 K with the calculated α."),
    sub("b-4. Radiative temperature sensitivity"),
    p("Find dTE/dε and the temperature change for a one-percent relative increase in ε."),
    sub("b-5. Non-radiative transfer"),
    p("Now use TA=245 K, TE=288 K and upward non-radiative flux JNR=k(TE−TA). Find ε and k in terms of the data and evaluate them."),
    sub("b-6a. Coupled sensitivity equations"),
    p("At fixed k, α and S0, differentiate the B5 balances with respect to ε to obtain two linear equations for X=dTE/dε and Y=dTA/dε."),
    sub("b-6b. Sensitivity with heat transfer"),
    p("Use those equations to find ΔTE for a one-percent relative rise in ε."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("The greenhouse effect"),
    sub("a-1. Solar constant"),
    p("Conserve solar luminosity over a sphere of radius d: \\(S_0=\\sigma T_S^4(R_S/d)^2=1.35\\times10^3\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}\\)."),
    sub("a-2. Airless blackbody Earth"),
    p("The absorbing disc has one quarter the radiating surface area. Thus \\(T_E=[S_0/(4\\sigma)]^{1/4}=T_S\\sqrt{R_S/(2d)}=278\\,\\mathrm K\\)."),
    sub("a-3. Wien equation"),
    p("Differentiating u with respect to λ gives \\(f(x)=5(1-e^{-x})-x=0\\). Any nonzero multiple yields the same root. Any nonzero constant multiple defines the same equation, so the derivation and equation use self-review rather than a unique expression key."),
    sub("a-4. Wien constant"),
    p("Iterate x=5(1−e⁻ˣ) starting near 5. The physical root is xm=4.965≈4.97. Hence \\(b=hc/(x_mk_B)\\simeq2.90\\times10^6\\,\\mathrm{nm\\,K}\\). The supplied rounded constants allow 2.89 to 2.90 million nm K."),
    sub("a-5. Solar and terrestrial peaks"),
    p("Use b/T, giving approximately 502 nm for the Sun and \\(1.04\\times10^{4}\\) nm for Earth."),
    sub("a-6. Equal-height rescaling"),
    p("At the maximum, x has the same value for both temperatures, and the peak exitance scales as T⁵. Thus \\(\\gamma=(d/R_S)^2(T_E/T_S)^5\\simeq1.20\\times10^{-2}\\)."),
    sub("b-1. Opaque infrared atmosphere"),
    p("Let F=(1−rA)S0/4. Surface balance is F+σTA⁴=σTE⁴ and atmospheric balance is σTE⁴=2σTA⁴. Thus \\(T_A=(F/\\sigma)^{1/4}=258\\,\\mathrm K\\) and \\(T_E=(2F/\\sigma)^{1/4}=307\\,\\mathrm K\\). The factor two counts upward and downward emission."),
    sub("b-2. Multiple-reflection albedo"),
    p("The direct reflected fraction is rA. The once-surface-reflected escaped fraction is (1−rA)²rE, with successive ratio rArE. Therefore \\[\\alpha=r_A+\\frac{(1-r_A)^2r_E}{1-r_Ar_E}=0.313.\\]"),
    sub("b-3a. Partially absorbing atmosphere"),
    p("For ε>0 atmospheric balance gives TE⁴=2TA⁴. Surface balance is (1−α)S0/4+εσTA⁴=σTE⁴. Hence \\[T_E=\\left[\\frac{(1-\\alpha)S_0}{2\\sigma(2-\\epsilon)}\\right]^{1/4}.\\]"),
    sub("b-3b. Present-temperature emissivity"),
    p("Rearrange B3a: \\(\\epsilon=2-(1-\\alpha)S_0/(2\\sigma T_E^4)\\simeq0.809\\)."),
    sub("b-4. Radiative temperature sensitivity"),
    p("At fixed S0 and α, \\(dT_E/d\\epsilon=T_E/[4(2-\\epsilon)]\\). A relative increase means Δε=0.01ε, not 0.01. At TE=288 K and ε≈0.809, ΔTE≈0.489 K."),
    sub("b-5. Non-radiative transfer"),
    p("Put F=(1−α)S0/4. The balances are \\(F+\\epsilon\\sigma T_A^4=\\sigma T_E^4+k(T_E-T_A)\\) and \\(\\epsilon\\sigma T_E^4+k(T_E-T_A)=2\\epsilon\\sigma T_A^4\\). Thus \\[\\epsilon=\\frac{\\sigma T_E^4-F}{\\sigma(T_E^4-T_A^4)},\\qquad k=\\frac{\\epsilon\\sigma(2T_A^4-T_E^4)}{T_E-T_A}.\\] Numerically ε≈0.850 and k≈0.361 W m⁻² K⁻¹."),
    sub("b-6a. Coupled sensitivity equations"),
    p("Differentiating the surface and atmospheric balances directly gives \\[(4\\sigma T_E^3+k)X-(4\\epsilon\\sigma T_A^3+k)Y=\\sigma T_A^4,\\] \\[(4\\epsilon\\sigma T_E^3+k)X-(8\\epsilon\\sigma T_A^3+k)Y=\\sigma(2T_A^4-T_E^4).\\] These are algebraically equivalent to the logarithmic-derivative equations in the official solution."),
    sub("b-6b. Sensitivity with heat transfer"),
    p("Solve the two linear equations at the B5 equilibrium and multiply X by 0.01ε. The result is approximately 0.524 K, with the source rounding range 0.521 to 0.528 K."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2024-t2"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Trapping ions and cooling atoms"),
    p("A ring of radius R in the xy plane has uniform positive line charge λ. A positive ion of charge Q and mass m is close to its centre. Ignore radiation. For dynamic trapping take λ=λ0+u cosΩt. In B, a trapped atom has transition energy ℏωA, excited lifetime τ and line half-width Γ estimated by energy-time uncertainty. Two counterpropagating lasers of frequency ωL slightly below ωA drive absorption at rate \\(s(\\omega)=s_L+\\alpha(\\omega-\\omega_L)\\) in the atom frame. Work to first order in v/c and assume m≫ℏωA/c² and sL≪αωL. An absorbed photon is re-emitted with nearly the same atom-frame frequency, with equal probability in either x direction. Use mH=\\(1.674\\times10^{-27}\\) kg, e=\\(1.602\\times10^{-19}\\) C, ε0=\\(8.854\\times10^{-12}\\) F/m, kB=\\(1.381\\times10^{-23}\\) J/K and ℏ=\\(1.055\\times10^{-34}\\) J s."),
    fig("batch8-paul-ring", "Original uniformly charged ring and a central ion with Cartesian axes."),
    sub("a-1a. Central electric field"),
    p("Find the field to first order in x/R,y/R,z/R."),
    sub("a-1b. Static stable frequencies"),
    p("Identify stable directions and their small-oscillation angular frequency."),
    sub("a-2. Alternating-field parameters"),
    p("For \\(\\ddot z=(k^2+a\\Omega^2\\cos\\Omega t)z\\), find k and a."),
    sub("a-3a. Fast component equation"),
    fig("batch8-paul-micromotion", "Original slow trajectory and rapid oscillations with enlarged inset."),
    p("Assume a≪1, Ω≫k and aΩ²≫k². Write z=p+q, with slowly varying p and small zero-mean rapidly varying q. Obtain the leading equation for q."),
    sub("a-3b. Micromotion solution"),
    p("Choose integration constants consistent with small zero-mean q and solve."),
    sub("a-4a. Averaged slow motion"),
    p("Find the effective equation for p."),
    sub("a-4b. Dynamic stability"),
    p("Find the condition for stable slow motion."),
    sub("a-5. Radiofrequency threshold"),
    p("Use λ0=\\(8\\times10^{-9}\\) C/m, R=0.10 m, Q=e and m=100mH. Calculate k and the limiting Ω for a=0.04."),
    sub("b-1. Lifetime width"),
    p("Estimate Γ from energy-time uncertainty."),
    sub("b-2a. Absorption and momentum rates"),
    fig("batch8-doppler-rate", "Original absorption line with laser frequency on its low-frequency slope."),
    p("For v>0 define + as the beam coming towards the atom from +x and − as the beam arriving from −x. Calculate s± and signed momentum rates π± in the atom frame."),
    sub("b-2b. Doppler friction"),
    p("Find the laboratory force in terms of v,kL=ωL/c,ℏ and α under the stated small-sL approximation."),
    sub("b-3. Recoil heating"),
    p("An atom initially at rest absorbs and re-emits one photon each τ. List the two final momentum outcomes and find mean heating power."),
    sub("b-4. Cooling equilibrium"),
    p("Calculate the signed mechanical cooling power, equilibrium ⟨v²⟩ and temperature."),
    sub("b-5. Temperature estimate"),
    p("For m=100mH, ωL=\\(2\\times10^{16}\\) rad/s, τ=\\(5\\times10^{-9}\\) s and α=4, estimate T."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Trapping ions and cooling atoms"),
    sub("a-1a. Central electric field"),
    p("On axis the ring charge 2πRλ gives \\(E_z=\\lambda Rz/[2\\epsilon_0(R^2+z^2)^{3/2}]\\simeq\\lambda z/(2\\epsilon_0R^2)\\). Symmetry and ∇·E=0 then give \\[\\mathbf E=\\frac\\lambda{4\\epsilon_0R^2}(-x,-y,2z).\\]"),
    sub("a-1b. Static stable frequencies"),
    p("For Qλ>0, x and y are restoring while z is unstable. Thus \\(\\omega_x=\\omega_y=\\sqrt{Q\\lambda/(4\\epsilon_0R^2m)}\\). The static ring cannot confine all directions."),
    sub("a-2. Alternating-field parameters"),
    p("Compare coefficients with QEz/m: \\(k=\\sqrt{Q\\lambda_0/(2\\epsilon_0R^2m)}\\), \\(a=Qu/(2\\epsilon_0R^2m\\Omega^2)\\). The field checks a."),
    sub("a-3a. Fast component equation"),
    p("On a fast cycle freeze p, neglect its acceleration and the small q in the driving force. Then \\(\\ddot q=pa\\Omega^2\\cos\\Omega t\\)."),
    sub("a-3b. Micromotion solution"),
    p("Integration gives \\(q=-pa\\cos\\Omega t+c_1t+c_2\\). Bounded micromotion requires c1=0 and zero mean requires c2=0, so \\(q=-pa\\cos\\Omega t\\)."),
    sub("a-4a. Averaged slow motion"),
    p("Substitute q and average, using ⟨cosΩt⟩=0 and ⟨cos²Ωt⟩=1/2. This gives \\(\\ddot p=(k^2-a^2\\Omega^2/2)p\\)."),
    sub("a-4b. Dynamic stability"),
    p("The coefficient must be negative: \\(a^2\\Omega^2/2>k^2\\), or Ω>√2 k/|a|. For a>0 this is Ω>√2 k/a. Equality is marginal, not a restoring oscillation."),
    sub("a-5. Radiofrequency threshold"),
    p("\\(k=\\sqrt{e\\lambda_0/(2\\epsilon_0R^2\\,100m_H)}\\simeq2.08\\times10^5\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}\\). Thus Ωmin≈\\(7.35\\times10^{6}\\) rad/s, rounded in the source to \\(7\\times10^{6}\\) rad/s. This is angular frequency, corresponding to about 1.17 MHz."),
    sub("b-1. Lifetime width"),
    p("Use ΔE τ≈ℏ and ΔE=ℏΓ, giving Γ≈1/τ in the source width convention. Factors associated with precise linewidth definitions are outside this estimate."),
    sub("b-2a. Absorption and momentum rates"),
    p("The frequencies are \\(\\omega_\\pm=\\omega_L(1\\pm v/c)\\), so \\(s_\\pm=s_L\\pm\\alpha\\omega_Lv/c\\). With k±=ω±/c, \\(\\pi_+=-\\hbar k_+s_+\\) and \\(\\pi_-=+\\hbar k_-s_-\\). Re-emission has zero mean momentum in this frame."),
    sub("b-2b. Doppler friction"),
    p("Adding momentum rates and retaining first-order terms gives \\(-2\\hbar k_L^2\\alpha v[1+s_L/(\\alpha\\omega_L)]\\). Neglect the bracket correction. To this order the lab force is \\(F=-2\\alpha\\hbar k_L^2v\\)."),
    sub("b-3. Recoil heating"),
    p("For incoming momentum +ℏkL, the final momentum is 0 or 2ℏkL with equal probability. The mean kinetic gain is ℏ²kL²/m. Thus \\(P_{in}=\\hbar^2k_L^2/(m\\tau)\\). The opposite incident direction gives the same energy."),
    sub("b-4. Cooling equilibrium"),
    p("\\(P_{cool}=Fv=-2\\alpha\\hbar k_L^2v^2\\). Balance the mean against recoil heating to obtain \\(\\langle v^2\\rangle=\\hbar/(2\\alpha m\\tau)\\). One-dimensional equipartition gives \\(T=\\hbar/(2\\alpha k_B\\tau)=\\hbar\\Gamma/(2\\alpha k_B)\\). The field checks T."),
    sub("b-5. Temperature estimate"),
    p("Substitution gives T≈\\(1.91\\times10^{-4}\\) K, about 0.2 mK. Mass and laser frequency cancel in this simplified balance."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2024-t3"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Black widow pulsar"),
    p("A circular binary contains point masses M1 and M2 separated by a. Its centre of mass is the origin of co-rotating xy coordinates, with M1 on negative x and M2 on positive x. The effective potential per test mass includes gravity and centrifugal potential. Mass transfer from M2 to M1 has small rate β>0, so orbits remain nearly circular. Assume conservative transfer and conserved total orbital angular momentum in A4. In A5 use a steady thin blackbody accretion disc close to M1, ignore M2's pull and gas heat capacity, and balance the local loss of circular orbital energy with radiation from both faces. For PSR J2215+5135 use MNS=2.27M☉, MS=0.33M☉, P=4.14 h, M☉=\\(1.98\\times10^{30}\\) kg, β=\\(9\\times10^{-10}\\) M☉/yr, G=\\(6.67\\times10^{-11}\\) SI and σ=\\(5.67\\times10^{-8}\\) SI. In B, a spherical isolated star obeys p=Kργ. Central values are pc and ρc, and outward acceleration is positive."),
    sub("a-1. Rotating potential"),
    p("Find φ(x,y) using M1,M2,G,a."),
    sub("a-2. Axial potential sketch"),
    p("For M1>M2 sketch φ(x,0)."),
    sub("a-3. Overflow point"),
    p("Only here let M2=M1/3. Find x0/a to two significant figures for gas overflow from M2 towards M1."),
    sub("a-4. Secular binary evolution"),
    p("Find da/dt and dP/dt under slow conservative mass transfer."),
    sub("a-5. Disc temperature law"),
    p("Find temperature at disc radius r."),
    sub("a-6. Observed-system estimate"),
    p("Calculate T at r=a/10 for the specified binary."),
    sub("a-7. Sudden mass loss"),
    p("An explosion leaves mass M1′ and relative speed v′ at separation a. Find the limiting v′ for a bound binary and the limiting M1′ if the explosion is isotropic."),
    sub("b-1. Central gravitational acceleration"),
    p("Find g(r) close to the centre."),
    sub("b-2. Density equilibrium equation"),
    p("Write \\(d[h_1(\\rho,r)d\\rho/dr]/dr+h_2(r)\\rho=0\\), identifying h1 and h2."),
    sub("b-3. Central length scale"),
    p("Construct r0=Gℓpcmρcn with length dimensions and no numerical prefactor."),
    sub("b-4. Dimensionless equation"),
    p("Put x=r/r0 and u=ρ/ρc. Find A1 and A2 in d[A1 du/dx]/dx+A2u=0."),
    sub("b-5. Analytic density for γ=2"),
    p("For γ=2 write u=f(x)/x and find f using a regular centre with u(0)=1."),
    sub("b-6. Infer equation-of-state exponent"),
    fig("batch8-star-density-gradient", "Original dimensionless du/dx plot including the near-origin slope and labelled axes."),
    p("Use the initial slope of the supplied du/dx plot to determine γ to three significant figures."),
    sub("b-7. Small homologous perturbation"),
    p("Near the centre let r̃=r[1+ε(t)] with |ε|≪1. Find ρ̃ and g̃ to first order."),
    sub("b-8. Perturbed shell acceleration"),
    p("Find d²r̃/dt² in terms of g̃,ρ̃,K,γ and the density gradient at fixed time."),
    sub("b-9. Radial stability criterion"),
    p("Derive the equation for ε, the limiting γ and the oscillation angular frequency."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Black widow pulsar"),
    sub("a-1. Rotating potential"),
    p("Set M=M1+M2, a1=M2a/M and a2=M1a/M, with Ω²=GM/a³. Then \\[\\varphi=-\\frac{GM_1}{\\sqrt{(x+a_1)^2+y^2}}-\\frac{GM_2}{\\sqrt{(x-a_2)^2+y^2}}-\\frac{GM}{2a^3}(x^2+y^2).\\] The centrifugal potential has a negative sign."),
    sub("a-2. Axial potential sketch"),
    p("Draw downward divergences on both sides of x=−a1 and x=a2, and φ→−∞ as x→±∞ because of centrifugal potential. There is one local maximum in each of the three intervals separated by the two masses, at the three collinear Lagrange points. The central maximum is closer to the smaller mass."),
    sub("a-3. Overflow point"),
    p("The masses lie at −a/4 and 3a/4. With ξ=x/a, solve \\(\\frac{3}{4(\\xi+1/4)^2}-\\frac{1}{4(3/4-\\xi)^2}-\\xi=0\\) between them. The inner stationary point is ξ≈0.3607, hence 0.36."),
    sub("a-4. Secular binary evolution"),
    p("Conserved angular momentum is \\(J=M_1M_2\\sqrt{Ga/(M_1+M_2)}\\). Using Ṁ1=β=−Ṁ2 gives \\[\\dot a=-2\\beta a(1/M_1-1/M_2),\\qquad\\dot P=-3\\beta P(1/M_1-1/M_2),\\] where \\(P=2\\pi\\sqrt{a^3/[G(M_1+M_2)]}\\). For M1>M2 the separation and period grow."),
    sub("a-5. Disc temperature law"),
    p("The circular specific orbital energy is −GM1/(2r). An annulus releases GM1βdr/(2r²). Its two radiating faces have area 4πrdr. Thus \\[T(r)=\\left(\\frac{GM_1\\beta}{8\\pi\\sigma r^3}\\right)^{1/4}.\\] This uses the local energy model requested here, without the angular-momentum-transport correction of a detailed viscous-disc theory."),
    sub("a-6. Observed-system estimate"),
    p("Kepler gives \\(a=[GP^2(M_{NS}+M_S)/(4\\pi^2)]^{1/3}\\). Convert β to kg/s and P to seconds, giving \\(T=[500\\pi M_{NS}\\beta/(\\sigma P^2(M_{NS}+M_S))]^{1/4}\\simeq9.0\\times10^3\\,\\mathrm K\\)."),
    sub("a-7. Sudden mass loss"),
    p("The new orbital energy is negative only for \\(v^{\\prime}<\\sqrt{2G(M_1^{\\prime}+M_2)/a}\\). Isotropic rapid ejection produces no kick, so v′²=G(M1+M2)/a. The condition is \\(M_1^{\\prime}>(M_1-M_2)/2\\). Equality in either condition is a parabolic, marginally unbound orbit."),
    sub("b-1. Central gravitational acceleration"),
    p("Enclosed mass is approximately 4πρcr³/3, hence \\(g=-4\\pi G\\rho_cr/3\\)."),
    sub("b-2. Density equilibrium equation"),
    p("Combine dp/dr=−GM(r)ρ/r² with M′=4πr²ρ and p=Kργ. Eliminate M to obtain \\[\\frac d{dr}\\left(r^2\\rho^{\\gamma-2}\\frac{d\\rho}{dr}\\right)+\\frac{4\\pi Gr^2}{K\\gamma}\\rho=0.\\] Thus h1=r²ργ−2 and h2=4πGr²/(Kγ)."),
    sub("b-3. Central length scale"),
    p("Matching mass, length and time exponents gives ℓ=−1/2, m=1/2, n=−1. Thus \\(r_0=\\sqrt{p_c/G}/\\rho_c\\)."),
    sub("b-4. Dimensionless equation"),
    p("Using pc=Kρcγ and the B3 scale, \\[\\frac d{dx}\\left(x^2u^{\\gamma-2}\\frac{du}{dx}\\right)+\\frac{4\\pi x^2}{\\gamma}u=0.\\] Thus A1=x²uγ−2 and A2=4πx²/γ."),
    sub("b-5. Analytic density for γ=2"),
    p("The equation reduces to f″+2πf=0. Regularity gives f(0)=0 and u(0)=f′(0)=1. Hence \\(f=\\sin(\\sqrt{2\\pi}x)/\\sqrt{2\\pi}\\)."),
    sub("b-6. Infer equation-of-state exponent"),
    p("Regularity imposes u′(0)=0. Expanding the dimensionless equation near zero gives \\(3u^{\\prime\\prime}(0)+4\\pi/\\gamma=0\\). The plotted initial slope of u′ is approximately −2.51, giving γ≈1.67. The official graphical acceptance interval is 1.64 to 1.70."),
    sub("b-7. Small homologous perturbation"),
    p("Mass conservation for a homologously expanded shell gives \\(\\tilde\\rho=\\rho(1+\\epsilon)^{-3}\\simeq\\rho(1-3\\epsilon)\\). Fixed enclosed mass gives \\(\\tilde g=g(1+\\epsilon)^{-2}\\simeq g(1-2\\epsilon)\\)."),
    sub("b-8. Perturbed shell acceleration"),
    p("Gravity plus pressure force per mass gives \\[\\ddot{\\tilde r}=\\tilde g-\\frac1{\\tilde\\rho}\\frac{\\partial\\tilde p}{\\partial\\tilde r}=\\tilde g-K\\gamma\\tilde\\rho^{\\gamma-2}\\frac{\\partial\\tilde\\rho}{\\partial\\tilde r}.\\]"),
    sub("b-9. Radial stability criterion"),
    p("The pressure acceleration scales as (1+ε)²−3γ, while gravity scales as (1+ε)⁻². Cancel equilibrium forces to find \\[\\ddot\\epsilon=\\frac gr(3\\gamma-4)\\epsilon=-\\frac{4\\pi G\\rho_c}{3}(3\\gamma-4)\\epsilon.\\] Stable restoring motion requires γ>4/3, and \\(\\omega=\\sqrt{4\\pi G\\rho_c(3\\gamma-4)/3}\\). At γ=4/3 this linear model is marginal."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2015-t1"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Fractional quantum Hall effect"),
    p("Electrons confined to a negligibly thin GaAs/AlGaAs layer have effective mass m*=0.067me. Ignore spin and random-potential scattering unless otherwise stated. A perpendicular field B points along +z and electric field is −Ey ŷ. The sample has length L, width W, area A=LW and N electrons. Take e>0 so electron charge is −e. A plateau occurs at RH=VH/I=3h/e². One vortex occupies a flux quantum h/e. For estimates use B=15 T, me=\\(9.1\\times10^{-31}\\) kg, Coulomb constant \\(9.0\\times10^{9}\\) SI, ε0=\\(8.854\\times10^{-12}\\) F/m, εr=13, e=\\(1.6\\times10^{-19}\\) C, h=\\(6.626\\times10^{-34}\\) J s and kB=\\(1.38\\times10^{-23}\\) J/K. In G a quantum point contact transmits independent carriers of positive charge magnitude e* at filling ν. The number nτ in time τ has Poisson law P(nτ=k)=λᵏe⁻λ/k!. Define noise as charge variance per time, rather than a spectral density with an extra factor of two."),
    fig("batch8-hall-setup", "Original Hall sample width, length, voltage leads and measured resistance plateaus."),
    sub("a.i. Lorentz equation"),
    p("Write the electron equation of motion."),
    sub("a.ii. Stationary drift speed"),
    p("Find the stationary speed vs."),
    sub("a.iii. Drift direction"),
    p("Which direction does the drift point?"),
    sub("b. Classical Hall resistance"),
    p("Express RH using total flux φ=BA and N."),
    sub("c. Filling fraction"),
    p("Find ν=N/Nφ at RH=3h/e²."),
    sub("d. Coulomb energy scaling"),
    p("Binding n>1 vortices to each electron increases their effective separation. Find α in the energy gain ΔU∝Bα at fixed filling."),
    sub("e. One quasihole"),
    p("At ν=1/3, find the increase ΔB that introduces exactly one positive quasihole of charge e/3 into area A."),
    sub("f.i. Thermal scale"),
    p("Calculate Eth at T=1.0 K."),
    sub("f.ii. Confinement kinetic scale"),
    p("Use uncertainty to estimate the kinetic energy of an electron localized in a vortex. Explain the length and momentum scales."),
    sub("g.i. Mean tunnelling current"),
    p("For the stated Poisson process find IB using λ,τ and carrier charge magnitude e*."),
    sub("g.ii. Shot-noise variance"),
    p("Find SI, the charge variance per time."),
    sub("g.iii. Fractional-charge signal"),
    p("Find SI/IB at ν=1/3."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Fractional quantum Hall effect"),
    sub("a.i. Lorentz equation"),
    p("\\(m^*d\\mathbf v/dt=-e(\\mathbf E+\\mathbf v\\times\\mathbf B)\\). The effective mass belongs in the inertial term and the electron charge has negative sign."),
    sub("a.ii. Stationary drift speed"),
    p("Set the Lorentz force to zero. The transverse drift is \\(\\mathbf v_s=\\mathbf E\\times\\mathbf B/B^2\\), of magnitude \\(v_s=E_y/B\\)."),
    sub("a.iii. Drift direction"),
    p("Because (−ŷ)×ẑ=−x̂, electron drift is along −x. Conventional current is along +x."),
    sub("b. Classical Hall resistance"),
    p("\\(I=Ne v_s/L=NeE_y/(BL)\\) and VH=EyW. Hence \\(R_H=BLW/(Ne)=\\phi/(Ne)\\), as a positive resistance magnitude."),
    sub("c. Filling fraction"),
    p("There are Nφ=φ/(h/e) flux quanta, so RH=(h/e²)/ν. Thus ν=1/3."),
    sub("d. Coulomb energy scaling"),
    p("The characteristic spacing is \\(l_0\\sim\\sqrt{h/(\\nu eB)}\\propto B^{-1/2}\\). Coulomb energy and its gain scale as e²/(4πε0εrl0), hence α=1/2."),
    sub("e. One quasihole"),
    p("One unbound vortex is one flux quantum. Thus AΔB=h/e, giving \\(\\Delta B=h/(eA)\\). The flux increment does not become h/(3e)."),
    sub("f.i. Thermal scale"),
    p("\\(E_{th}=k_BT=1.38\\times10^{-23}\\,\\mathrm J\\)."),
    sub("f.ii. Confinement kinetic scale"),
    p("A single-vortex length is \\(l_0\\sim\\sqrt{h/(eB)}=1.66\\times10^{-8}\\,\\mathrm m\\). Taking Δp~h/l0 gives \\(E_k\\sim h eB/(2m^*)\\simeq1.3\\times10^{-20}\\,\\mathrm J\\). Using ℏ instead changes numerical factors. This is an order-of-magnitude result, and the source accepts a factor 2π variation, so it requires self-review."),
    sub("g.i. Mean tunnelling current"),
    p("From ΣkP(k)=λ, the mean transmitted charge is e*λ. Therefore \\(I_B=e^*\\lambda/\\tau\\). The field uses q for e*."),
    sub("g.ii. Shot-noise variance"),
    p("The Poisson second moment is λ²+λ, so Var(nτ)=λ. Thus \\(S_I=(e^*)^2\\lambda/\\tau\\). The field uses q for e*."),
    sub("g.iii. Fractional-charge signal"),
    p("The ratio is e*=νe=e/3≈\\(5.33\\times10^{-20}\\) C. This follows the variance-per-time noise convention specified in the problem."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2015-t3"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Fabry-Perot etalon and Zeeman splitting"),
    p("An air-tunable Fabry-Perot cavity has inner-surface reflectivity R=0.90 and spacing t=1.00 cm. Its transmission is \\(T=[1+F\\sin^2(\\delta/2)]^{-1}\\), \\(F=4R/(1-R)^2\\), \\(\\delta=4\\pi nt\\cos\\theta/\\lambda\\). A sodium lamp, collimator, D1-only filter, etalon and f=30.0 cm imaging lens produce concentric fringes observed by microscope. D1 has λ=589.6 nm. Outer glass faces do not participate in cavity reflections. Use h=\\(6.626\\times10^{-34}\\) J s, e=\\(1.6\\times10^{-19}\\) C, me=\\(9.1\\times10^{-31}\\) kg and c=\\(3.0\\times10^{8}\\) m/s. Vacuum means n=1. Zeeman shifts are mjgkμBB with μB=he/(4πme). Both upper ²P1/2 and lower ²S1/2 levels have J=1/2. Their full splitting gaps are ΔE1<ΔE2. The source diagrams define the four transitions and ring labels."),
    fig("batch8-etalon-apparatus", "Original sodium source, collimator, filter, pressure-tunable etalon, imaging lens and microscope."),
    sub("a.i. First three orders"),
    p("For vacuum find interference orders mi of the first three bright rings counted outwards from the centre."),
    sub("a.ii. Ring angles"),
    p("Calculate their incidence angles."),
    sub("a.iii. Ring diameters"),
    p("Calculate their image diameters Di."),
    sub("b.i. Phase linewidth"),
    fig("batch8-etalon-linewidth", "Original transmission peaks and phase full width at half maximum."),
    p("Find the phase FWHM ε near a transmission maximum."),
    sub("b.ii. Resolving power"),
    p("Near normal incidence find λ/Δλ under the criterion that the peak phase separation equals ε."),
    sub("c. Air refractive index"),
    p("Slow admission from vacuum to atmospheric pressure produces ten new fringes from the centre. Estimate nair."),
    sub("d. Four Zeeman frequencies"),
    fig("batch8-zeeman-transitions", "Original four labelled transitions between the two split sodium doublets."),
    p("Write νa,νb,νc,νd. The upper-to-lower mj pairs are a:(−1/2,+1/2), b:(+1/2,+1/2), c:(−1/2,−1/2), d:(+1/2,−1/2)."),
    sub("e. Splittings from ring diameters"),
    fig("batch8-zeeman-rings", "Original unsplit interference rings and four split sub-fringes with diameter labels."),
    p("For the four rings D1<D2<D3<D4 corresponding to a,b,c,d at the same order, derive ΔE1 and ΔE2 in terms of the measured diameters."),
    sub("f. Measured Landé factors"),
    p("At B=0.1 T the diameters are 3.88,4.05,4.35,4.51 mm. Find gk1 and gk2."),
    sub("g. Solar magnetic field"),
    p("The shortest-to-longest sodium D1 wavelength separation in a solar region is 0.012 nm. Find B using the Landé factors."),
    sub("h.i. LED transmitted comb"),
    p("At normal incidence a vacuum etalon receives an LED band centred at 650 nm with width 20 nm. Estimate the number of transmitted lines."),
    sub("h.ii. Individual comb linewidth"),
    p("Find the frequency FWHM of each transmitted line."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Fabry-Perot etalon and Zeeman splitting"),
    sub("a.i. First three orders"),
    p("Bright transmission requires 2ntcosθ=mλ. Since 2t/λ=33921.3026, the highest accessible integer is 33921. Thus m1=33921, m2=33920 and m3=33919."),
    sub("a.ii. Ring angles"),
    p("Use \\(\\theta_i=\\arccos(m_i\\lambda/(2t))\\), obtaining approximately 0.242°, 0.502° and 0.668°. The source rounding gives 0.241°, 0.502° and 0.667°."),
    sub("a.iii. Ring diameters"),
    p("\\(D_i=2f\\tan\\theta_i\\simeq2f\\theta_i\\), with angles in radians. The diameters are approximately 2.53, 5.26 and 6.99 mm."),
    sub("b.i. Phase linewidth"),
    p("At half maximum Fsin²(ε/4)=1. Thus \\(\\epsilon=4\\arcsin(1/\\sqrt F)\\simeq4/\\sqrt F=0.211\\,\\mathrm{rad}\\)."),
    sub("b.ii. Resolving power"),
    p("\\(|\\Delta\\delta|=4\\pi nt|\\Delta\\lambda|/\\lambda^2\\). Thus \\(\\lambda/\\Delta\\lambda=4\\pi nt/(\\lambda\\epsilon)\\simeq\\pi nt\\sqrt F/\\lambda=1.01\\times10^6\\)."),
    sub("c. Air refractive index"),
    p("Each new centre crossing increases optical order by one. Hence \\(2t(n_{air}-1)/\\lambda\\simeq10\\), giving \\(n_{air}\\simeq1+10\\lambda/(2t)=1.000295\\). The fractional initial order and endpoint counting limit the precision of this estimate."),
    sub("d. Four Zeeman frequencies"),
    p("With ν0=c/λ, \\[\\nu_a=\\nu_0-\\frac{\\Delta E_1+\\Delta E_2}{2h},\\quad\\nu_b=\\nu_0-\\frac{\\Delta E_2-\\Delta E_1}{2h},\\] \\[\\nu_c=\\nu_0+\\frac{\\Delta E_2-\\Delta E_1}{2h},\\quad\\nu_d=\\nu_0+\\frac{\\Delta E_1+\\Delta E_2}{2h}.\\] Each level is displaced by half its full gap."),
    sub("e. Splittings from ring diameters"),
    p("Paraxial interference gives \\(|\\Delta\\lambda|/\\lambda=|D_j^2-D_i^2|/(8f^2)\\), and \\(|\\Delta\\nu|=c|\\Delta\\lambda|/\\lambda^2\\). Thus \\[\\Delta E_1=\\frac{hc}{8\\lambda f^2}(D_2^2-D_1^2)=\\frac{hc}{8\\lambda f^2}(D_4^2-D_3^2),\\] \\[\\Delta E_2=\\frac{hc}{8\\lambda f^2}(D_4^2-D_2^2)=\\frac{hc}{8\\lambda f^2}(D_3^2-D_1^2).\\] Experimental rounding makes alternative pairs slightly different."),
    sub("f. Measured Landé factors"),
    p("Divide the E-derived gaps by μBB. Pairs (D2,D1) and (D4,D2) give gk1≈0.68 and gk2≈1.99. Alternative pairs give about 0.72 and 1.95. These are consistent with theoretical 2/3 and 2 within the supplied measurement precision."),
    sub("g. Solar magnetic field"),
    p("The full energy span is (gk1+gk2)μBB≈hcΔλ/λ². Thus \\(B=4\\pi m_ec\\Delta\\lambda/[e\\lambda^2(g_{k1}+g_{k2})]\\). Using the source sum 2.67 gives B≈0.277 T, or 2770 gauss."),
    sub("h.i. LED transmitted comb"),
    p("The free spectral range is c/(2t)=\\(1.5\\times10^{10}\\) Hz. The LED width is approximately cΔλ/λ²=\\(1.42\\times10^{13}\\) Hz, giving N≈2tΔλ/λ²≈947. The source rounds the bandwidth first and reports 946. Exact endpoint counting depends on the specified band edges."),
    sub("h.ii. Individual comb linewidth"),
    p("\\(\\Delta\\nu=c\\epsilon/(4\\pi nt)\\simeq c/(\\pi nt\\sqrt F)\\simeq5.0\\times10^8\\,\\mathrm{Hz}\\). This is the individual linewidth, not the 15 GHz free spectral range."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2017-t3"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Cosmic inflation"),
    p("Model a homogeneous isotropic universe by an expanding uniform-density sphere of radius R(t)=a(t)Rs. Its mass-equivalent energy density is ρ, so total energy density is ρc². The first Friedmann equation is \\(H^2=A_1\\rho-kc^2/(R_s^2a^2)\\), where H=ȧ/a and k is +1,0,−1 for closed, flat and open geometry. Adiabatic evolution has \\(\\dot\\rho+A_2(\\rho+p/c^2)H=0\\), with p=wρc². Present values have subscript 0 and a0=1. Define critical mass density consistently with the first equation as ρc=H²/A1 and Ω=ρ/ρc. The source prints an extra c² in one definition of critical density, while its worked solution uses this dimensionally consistent definition. During scalar-field inflation, \\(\\ddot\\phi+3H\\dot\\phi=-V^{\\prime}\\), \\(H^2=(\\dot\\phi^2/2+V)/(3M_{pl}^2)\\). Slow roll neglects φ̈ and kinetic energy. Define \\(\\epsilon=-\\dot H/H^2\\), \\(\\delta=-\\ddot\\phi/(H\\dot\\phi)\\), ηV=δ+ε and dN=dln a=Hdt, with N=0 at the end. Thus N is negative before the end. Part D retains the source's requested numerical N=60 and discusses this sign convention explicitly."),
    fig("batch8-cosmic-sphere", "Original homogeneous expanding sphere and reference and evolving radii."),
    sub("a.1. First Friedmann coefficient"),
    p("Use Newtonian gravity for a boundary test mass to determine A1."),
    sub("a.2. Adiabatic continuity"),
    p("Use the first law to determine A2."),
    sub("a.3. Equations of state"),
    p("Find w for radiation, nonrelativistic matter and constant energy density."),
    sub("a.4. Flat-universe scale factors"),
    p("For k=0 find a(t) in the three cases. Use a(0)=0 for radiation and matter, and a(t0)=1 for constant density."),
    sub("a.5. Curvature-density relation"),
    p("Express k through Ω,H,a,Rs and c."),
    sub("a.6. Geometry ranges"),
    p("Give the density-ratio range for each k."),
    sub("b.1. Flatness problem"),
    p("Find the time dependence of Ω−1 in radiation- and matter-dominated nearly flat universes."),
    sub("b.2. Inflationary flattening"),
    p("For a constant-density era and |Ω−1|≪1, find Ω−1 as a function of time."),
    sub("b.3. Equivalent inflation conditions"),
    p("Show that constant-density domination produces negative pressure, accelerated expansion and a decreasing comoving Hubble radius."),
    sub("b.4. Slow-roll inflation bound"),
    p("Show that decreasing (aH)⁻¹ means ε<1."),
    sub("c.1. Potential slow-roll formulas"),
    p("Estimate ε,ηV and dN/dφ using V,V′,V″."),
    sub("d.1. End of monomial inflation"),
    p("For V=Λ⁴(φ/Mpl)ⁿ with integer n and positive field, find φend when ε=1."),
    sub("d.2. Observable constraints and e-fold sign"),
    p("Using r=16ε and ns=1+2ηV−6ε at the start, express r and ns in N,n. Compare integer n to ns=0.968±0.006 and r<0.12 at the source's N=60."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Cosmic inflation"),
    sub("a.1. First Friedmann coefficient"),
    p("For enclosed M=4πR³ρ/3, integrate R̈=−GM/R² to Ṙ²/2−GM/R=constant. Divide by R² and absorb the integration constant into curvature. This yields \\(A_1=8\\pi G/3\\)."),
    sub("a.2. Adiabatic continuity"),
    p("For E=ρVc² and V∝a³, dE=−pdV gives \\(\\dot\\rho+3H(\\rho+p/c^2)=0\\). Therefore A2=3."),
    sub("a.3. Equations of state"),
    p("The continuity equation gives ρ∝a⁻³⁽¹⁺ʷ⁾. Radiation dilutes in number as a⁻³ and photon energy as a⁻¹, giving w=1/3. Nonrelativistic rest energy dilutes as a⁻³, giving w=0. Constant energy density has w=−1 and negative pressure."),
    sub("a.4. Flat-universe scale factors"),
    p("Radiation has \\(a=(2H_0t)^{1/2}\\). Matter has \\(a=(3H_0t/2)^{2/3}\\). Constant density has \\(a=e^{H_0(t-t_0)}\\). In the first two cases the time origin is the Big Bang and H0 refers to the epoch with a=1."),
    sub("a.5. Curvature-density relation"),
    p("Rearranging the first Friedmann equation gives \\(k=(\\Omega-1)H^2a^2R_s^2/c^2\\). The source alternates Rs and R0 for the fixed reference radius."),
    sub("a.6. Geometry ranges"),
    p("For positive density, k=+1 corresponds to Ω>1, k=0 to Ω=1, and k=−1 to 0<Ω<1."),
    sub("b.1. Flatness problem"),
    p("The exact curvature relation is Ω−1=kc²/(Rs²a²H²). In a nearly flat era with a∝tᵖ and H=p/t this scales as t²⁻²ᵖ. It grows as t in radiation domination and t²ᐟ³ in matter domination. The expansion laws are the nearly flat approximation used in this argument."),
    sub("b.2. Inflationary flattening"),
    p("H is approximately constant and a∝exp(Ht), so \\(\\Omega(t)-1=[\\Omega(t_i)-1]e^{-2H(t-t_i)}\\). Curvature deviations decay exponentially."),
    sub("b.3. Equivalent inflation conditions"),
    p("Constant density implies p=−ρc². Differentiating Friedmann and using continuity gives \\(\\ddot a/a=-4\\pi G(\\rho+3p/c^2)/3\\), hence positive acceleration. Since aH=ȧ, \\(d[(aH)^{-1}]/dt=-\\ddot a/\\dot a^2<0\\)."),
    sub("b.4. Slow-roll inflation bound"),
    p("\\(d[(aH)^{-1}]/dt=-(1+\\dot H/H^2)/a=-(1-\\epsilon)/a\\). With a>0 this is negative exactly when ε<1."),
    sub("c.1. Potential slow-roll formulas"),
    p("From \\(\\dot H=-\\dot\\phi^2/(2M_{pl}^2)\\), H²≈V/(3Mpl²) and 3Hφ̇≈−V′, obtain \\[\\epsilon\\simeq\\frac{M_{pl}^2}{2}\\left(\\frac{V^{\\prime}}V\\right)^2,\\quad\\eta_V\\simeq M_{pl}^2\\frac{V^{\\prime\\prime}}V,\\quad\\frac{dN}{d\\phi}\\simeq-\\frac{V}{M_{pl}^2V^{\\prime}}.\\] These expressions require a monotonic field and the stated slow-roll approximation."),
    sub("d.1. End of monomial inflation"),
    p("\\(\\epsilon=n^2M_{pl}^2/(2\\phi^2)\\), giving \\(\\phi_{end}=|n|M_{pl}/\\sqrt2\\). The usual positive-n rolling branch has n>0. The n=0 constant potential never reaches ε=1 by this mechanism."),
    sub("d.2. Observable constraints and e-fold sign"),
    p("Integrating dN/dφ gives \\(\\phi^2=M_{pl}^2(n^2/2-2nN)\\). Therefore \\[r=\\frac{16n}{n-4N},\\qquad n_s=1-\\frac{2(n+2)}{n-4N}.\\] With the literal N=+60 used by the source, ns=0.968 gives n≈−5.93, but n=−6 gives r≈0.390>0.12. No integer fits both constraints. This positive N conflicts with dN=Hdt and N=0 at the end for a pre-end epoch. If 60 means remaining e-folds, use N=−60 here, or replace denominators by n+240. Then n=1 gives r≈0.0664, ns≈0.9751, just outside the stated tilt interval, and n=2 gives r≈0.132>0.12. Again no positive integer satisfies both. The sign issue is kept explicit and this comparison uses self-review."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["ipho-2022-t1"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Permanent magnets"),
    p("Uniformly magnetized permanent magnets have magnetization J=1.5 T/μ0, where μ0=\\(4\\pi\\times10^{-7}\\) SI. A uniformly magnetized body has moment m=JV. Outside a spherical magnet its field is the point-dipole field \\(\\mathbf B=\\mu_0(2\\mathbf m_\\parallel-\\mathbf m_\\perp)/(4\\pi r^3)\\). A uniformly magnetized cylinder can be represented by a surface current along its curved side. In A1-A3 the cylinders have diameter d=2.0 cm and height h=2.0 mm, with magnetization along their axes. In A4-A5 spherical magnets have diameter δ=5.0 mm and material density 7500 kg/m³. For high-permeability bodies in B, take μ≫μ0 and retain finite closed magnetic flux paths. In C use only nearest-neighbour dipole interactions for equal spheres in a plane. An illustrative source sketch accompanies each geometric construction."),
    fig("batch8-coaxial-magnets", "Original cylindrical magnets with parallel axial magnetization, diameter d, thickness h and separation L."),
    sub("a.1. Distant coaxial magnets"),
    p("Two coaxial cylindrical magnets have centres L=20 cm apart and parallel moments. Find the attractive force."),
    sub("a.2. Equivalent circulating current"),
    p("Find the total side current I representing one cylinder."),
    sub("a.3. Closely spaced cylinders"),
    p("For centre separation L=5.0 mm estimate the force, using h≪L≪d."),
    sub("a.4. Hanging sphere chain"),
    p("A vertical chain of touching equal spheres hangs from its top sphere. Estimate its maximum length, including all pair attractions across the top link."),
    sub("a.5. Field near a long chain end"),
    fig("batch8-chain-end", "Original long magnetic sphere chain endpoint O and observation point P at distance r and angle theta."),
    p("For a chain of length ℓ, find field magnitude and direction at P a distance r from endpoint O, with ℓ≫r and r sinθ≫δ, where θ is the angle to the chain axis."),
    sub("b.1. Field lines at a permeable plate"),
    p("A spherical magnet has its moment normal to a large thick plate of very high permeability, with sphere centre one diameter δ above the surface. Sketch complete field-line paths, including the region inside the plate and the magnet. Show the three families represented in the source answer sheet: a line staying outside the plate, a line passing through its interior and a line entering near the magnet and returning far away. Explain boundary refraction and closure."),
    sub("b.2. Contact force and orientation"),
    p("The sphere touches the high-permeability plate. Find stable moment orientation and the attractive force."),
    sub("b.3. Magnet between circular plates"),
    p("A cylinder of diameter d and height h is sandwiched between high-permeability circular plates of diameter D=2d. Find the uniform inner and return-annulus fields and the force on each plate. Neglect external leakage and take h≪d."),
    sub("c.1. Square lattice"),
    fig("batch8-square-spheres", "Original square lattice of touching spherical magnets."),
    p("For touching spheres on a square lattice, identify a stable collective moment arrangement and the energy needed to remove one sphere to infinity. Consider four nearest neighbours only."),
    sub("c.2. Triangular lattice"),
    fig("batch8-triangular-spheres", "Original triangular lattice of touching spherical magnets."),
    p("Repeat for the triangular lattice with six nearest neighbours."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Permanent magnets"),
    sub("a.1. Distant coaxial magnets"),
    p("Each moment is \\(m=J\\pi d^2h/4=0.75\\,\\mathrm{A\\,m^2}\\). Differentiate the axial dipole interaction energy to obtain \\(F=3\\mu_0m^2/(2\\pi L^4)=2.11\\times10^{-4}\\,\\mathrm N\\)."),
    sub("a.2. Equivalent circulating current"),
    p("Bound surface current density is J, so the total circulating current across cylinder height is \\(I=Jh\\simeq2.39\\times10^3\\,\\mathrm A\\)."),
    sub("a.3. Closely spaced cylinders"),
    p("Locally the side currents are parallel wires separated by L. Integrate the force per length μ0I²/(2πL) around circumference πd: \\(F\\simeq\\mu_0I^2d/(2L)\\simeq14\\,\\mathrm N\\). This neglects curvature and finite height."),
    sub("a.4. Hanging sphere chain"),
    p("For one sphere m=Jπδ³/6 and mass M=ρπδ³/6. The top sphere supports the lower chain through \\(F=3\\mu_0m^2\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}n^{-4}/(2\\pi\\delta^4)=\\mu_0m^2\\pi^3/(60\\delta^4)\\). Equate to NMg, giving roughly 1320 spheres and length 6.6 m. This is an ideal straight-chain estimate ignoring mechanical instability."),
    sub("a.5. Field near a long chain end"),
    p("The moments per unit length m/δ form a thin solenoid. Away from its remote end the near endpoint acts as a magnetic pole of strength m/δ. Thus \\[B=\\frac{\\mu_0m}{4\\pi\\delta r^2}=\\frac{\\mu_0J\\delta^2}{24r^2},\\] independent of θ. Field is radial from O, outward for a north endpoint and inward for a south endpoint. The conditions permit a continuum approximation and neglect of the far endpoint."),
    sub("b.1. Field lines at a permeable plate"),
    p("Normal B and tangential H are continuous. If α and β are the angles to the surface normal inside and outside the plate, \\(\\tan\\alpha=(\\mu/\\mu_0)\\tan\\beta\\). For μ≫μ0 the external line meets the surface almost normally. Each magnetic line must close. Lines entering the plate return through it and emerge elsewhere, possibly very far away. They must not terminate in the plate or leave through its distant lower face in the depicted thick-plate approximation. The source statement refers to three points on a separate answer sheet, not retained with its PDF. Here those path families are stated explicitly, and the source worked sketch is supplied in the solution."),
    fig("batch8-plate-lines", "Original worked field-line paths around the spherical magnet and through the high-permeability plate."),
    sub("b.2. Contact force and orientation"),
    p("An image magnet mirrored in the plane has parallel normal moment, with centre separation δ. Either polarity normal to the plate is a stable orientation. The attractive force is \\(F=3\\mu_0m^2/(2\\pi\\delta^4)\\simeq5.9\\,\\mathrm N\\)."),
    sub("b.3. Magnet between circular plates"),
    p("Flux conservation gives B1d²=B2(D²−d²), so B1=3B2. Ampère circulation with side current Jh gives B1+B2=μ0J. Hence B1=3μ0J/4=1.125 T and opposite return field has magnitude B2=μ0J/4=0.375 T. Maxwell pressure integrated over the inner disc and outer annulus gives \\[F=\\frac\\pi{8\\mu_0}[d^2B_1^2+(D^2-d^2)B_2^2]=\\frac{3\\pi\\mu_0J^2d^2}{32}\\simeq210\\,\\mathrm N.\\] Field energy alone at fixed permanent magnetization misses the magnet's interaction energy and can give a wrong force sign."),
    sub("c.1. Square lattice"),
    p("One stable pattern has parallel moments along each horizontal row and opposite moments in adjacent rows. The nearest-neighbour field at a sphere is \\(B=3\\mu_0m/(2\\pi\\delta^3)\\), aligned with its moment. Removing the sphere breaks its interactions and costs \\(W=mB=3\\mu_0m^2/(2\\pi\\delta^3)\\simeq29\\,\\mathrm{mJ}\\). Do not divide this single-removal energy by two. The source also accepts an appropriate vortex-like stable pattern, so the sketch is self-reviewed."),
    sub("c.2. Triangular lattice"),
    p("A stable arrangement has all moments parallel to one lattice direction. Sum the six dipole fields, whose transverse components cancel, to get \\(B=3\\mu_0m/(4\\pi\\delta^3)\\). The removal energy is \\(W=3\\mu_0m^2/(4\\pi\\delta^3)\\simeq15\\,\\mathrm{mJ}\\)."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2016-t3"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Magnetic field effects on superconductors"),
    p("Electrons in a superconductor can lower their energy by Δ per electron by forming an opposite-spin, opposite-momentum Cooper pair. First model an electron as a uniformly charged ring with charge −e, mass m and radius r. Its angular velocity points along the ring normal n̂. The Bohr magneton is μB=eℏ/(2me)=\\(5.788\\times10^{-5}\\) eV/T. In B write the common two-electron kinetic energy as K0=p1²/(2me)+p2²/(2me). Ignore orbital effects there. In C ignore spins and minimize the normalized real-wavefunction functional \\[F=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\psi\\left(-\\alpha\\psi-\\frac{\\hbar^2}{4m_e}\\psi^{\\prime\\prime}+\\frac{e^2B_z^2x^2}{m_e}\\psi\\right)dx,\\qquad\\alpha>0.\\] In D, spin-orbit coupling gives opposite internal fields +Bz ẑ and −Bz ẑ to opposite-momentum electrons. These internal fields act on spin only. An external in-plane field Bx x̂ is perpendicular to them, and orbital effects of this in-plane field are ignored."),
    fig("batch8-superconductor-ring", "Original charged rotating ring, angular velocity, radius and coordinate axes."),
    sub("a1. Ring angular momentum"),
    p("Find the magnitude and direction of angular momentum for angular speed ω."),
    sub("a2. Orbital magnetic moment"),
    p("Relate ring magnetic moment to its angular momentum."),
    sub("a3. Orientation energy"),
    p("If n̂ makes angle θ to a uniform field Bz ẑ, find U with U=0 at θ=π/2."),
    sub("a4. Classical spin estimate"),
    p("Use the ring gyromagnetic relation for spin projection ±ℏ/2. Find energies for spins parallel and antiparallel to B."),
    sub("a5. Quantum spin energies"),
    p("Quantum mechanics doubles the A4 values. Give both energies at B=1 T and use this convention subsequently."),
    sub("b1. Pair energy"),
    p("Find ES for a Cooper pair in a uniform field when only spin is affected."),
    sub("b2. Normal-state minimum"),
    p("Find the minimum energy EN of two unpaired electrons in an in-plane field Bx>0."),
    sub("b3. Paramagnetic threshold"),
    p("Find BP above which the normal state is preferred in this pair model."),
    sub("c1. Optimal Gaussian"),
    p("For \\(\\psi=(2\\lambda/\\pi)^{1/4}e^{-\\lambda x^2}\\), find λ minimizing F. You may use Gaussian normalization and ⟨x²⟩=1/(4λ)."),
    sub("c2. Orbital critical field"),
    p("Find the critical |Bz| where the superconducting state is no longer energetically favoured."),
    sub("d1. Ising pair energy"),
    fig("batch8-ising-internal", "Original opposite internal spin-orbit magnetic fields for opposite-momentum electrons."),
    p("Find EI for the opposite-momentum pair in the internal fields of magnitude Bz>0."),
    sub("d2. Normal energy in crossed fields"),
    p("Find the minimum normal-state energy E∥ under external Bx."),
    sub("d3. Enhanced Ising threshold"),
    p("Find BI such that E∥<EI for |Bx|>BI."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Magnetic field effects on superconductors"),
    sub("a1. Ring angular momentum"),
    p("The ring inertia is mr². Thus \\(\\mathbf L=mr^2\\omega\\hat{\\mathbf n}\\), with direction given by the right-hand rule."),
    sub("a2. Orbital magnetic moment"),
    p("The signed current is I=−eω/(2π), so \\(\\boldsymbol\\mu=I\\pi r^2\\hat{\\mathbf n}=-e\\mathbf L/(2m)\\). The magnetic moment is antiparallel to angular momentum."),
    sub("a3. Orientation energy"),
    p("Integrate magnetic torque or use \\(U=-\\boldsymbol\\mu\\cdot\\mathbf B\\). Thus \\(U=er^2\\omega B_z\\cos\\theta/2\\). The positive sign follows because the ring charge is negative."),
    sub("a4. Classical spin estimate"),
    p("With the classical factor one, \\(U_{up}=+\\mu_BB/2\\) and \\(U_{down}=-\\mu_BB/2\\). These are estimates before the quantum spin factor is applied."),
    sub("a5. Quantum spin energies"),
    p("The corrected energies are U′up=+μBB=+\\(5.788\\times10^{-5}\\) eV and U′down=−μBB=−\\(5.788\\times10^{-5}\\) eV. Magnetic moment parallel to B means spin antiparallel to B."),
    sub("b1. Pair energy"),
    p("Opposite spin Zeeman energies cancel, so \\(E_S=K_0-2\\Delta\\)."),
    sub("b2. Normal-state minimum"),
    p("Both magnetic moments align with the field, lowering each electron energy by μBBx. Thus \\(E_N=K_0-2\\mu_BB_x\\)."),
    sub("b3. Paramagnetic threshold"),
    p("EN<ES when 2μBBx>2Δ. Thus \\(B_P=\\Delta/\\mu_B\\). This simplified two-electron criterion differs from the full condensation-energy Pauli limit by a factor √2."),
    sub("c1. Optimal Gaussian"),
    p("The expectation is \\(F=-\\alpha+\\hbar^2\\lambda/(4m_e)+e^2B_z^2/(4m_e\\lambda)\\). Set its derivative to zero with λ>0, obtaining \\(\\lambda=e|B_z|/\\hbar\\). The second derivative is positive. The field checks Bz>0."),
    sub("c2. Orbital critical field"),
    p("At the minimum \\(F_{min}=-\\alpha+e\\hbar|B_z|/(2m_e)\\). Setting it to zero gives \\(B_c=2m_e\\alpha/(e\\hbar)=\\alpha/\\mu_B\\)."),
    sub("d1. Ising pair energy"),
    p("The two spins are opposite but each magnetic moment aligns with its own opposite internal field. Thus \\(E_I=K_0-2\\Delta-2\\mu_BB_z\\)."),
    sub("d2. Normal energy in crossed fields"),
    p("The two total fields are (Bx,0,±Bz), both of magnitude \\(\\sqrt{B_x^2+B_z^2}\\). Align each magnetic moment with its own total field, giving \\(E_\\parallel=K_0-2\\mu_B\\sqrt{B_x^2+B_z^2}\\)."),
    sub("d3. Enhanced Ising threshold"),
    p("At equality \\(\\sqrt{B_I^2+B_z^2}=B_z+\\Delta/\\mu_B\\). Therefore \\[B_I=\\frac{\\sqrt{\\Delta^2+2\\Delta\\mu_BB_z}}{\\mu_B}.\\] It can greatly exceed BP when the internal spin-orbit field is large."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2017-t2"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Evolution of a supermassive black-hole binary"),
    p("Use Newtonian point masses except for the supplied gravitational radiation law. Each black hole has mass M≫m, every star has m=M☉, and the holes eventually form a binary with each circular orbital radius a, separation 2a. Use G=\\(6.67\\times10^{-11}\\) SI, c=\\(3.0\\times10^{8}\\) m/s, M☉=\\(2.0\\times10^{30}\\) kg, 1 pc=\\(3.1\\times10^{16}\\) m and cosmic age tH=13.7 Gy. Give distances in pc and times in Gy. Estimates may differ by up to a factor ten, while tasks saying find require exact expressions within the given model. In A stars initially rest with uniform density ρ=mn in a galaxy of size R. The hole moves at v and b1=GM/v². In B stars far from the binary have speed σ≪vbin and density ρ. A star reaching distance about a is ejected at speed about vbin. In C use exactly the supplied radiation model \\(\\dot E=-(1024/5)(G/c^5)(\\omega^3I)^2\\), with I=2Ma² and ω=vbin/a. This coefficient is part of the exercise model. In D set M=\\(10^{8}\\)M☉, σ=200 km/s, R=20 kpc and stellar density ρ(r)=σ²/(4πGr²)."),
    sub("a.1. Weak scattering coefficient"),
    fig("batch8-black-hole-scattering", "Original stellar deflection with impact parameter b and closest distance rm."),
    p("For b≫b1, find k in the deflection angle α=kb1/b."),
    sub("a.2. Longitudinal impulse"),
    p("With +x along the hole velocity, find the momentum Δpx transferred to the hole in one encounter."),
    sub("a.3. Dynamical friction"),
    p("Integrate encounters from b1 to R to estimate the drag force."),
    sub("a.4. Coulomb logarithm"),
    p("Calculate lnΛ=ln(R/b1) for M=\\(10^{8}\\)M☉, R=20 kpc and v=200 km/s."),
    sub("b.1. Circular binary energy"),
    p("Find each hole's speed vbin and total binary energy E."),
    sub("b.2. Gravitational focusing"),
    p("A star of asymptotic speed σ passes a fixed point mass M2 with closest approach rm. Find exact impact parameter b."),
    sub("b.3. Collision interval"),
    p("Estimate the interval Δt between successive strong binary-star interactions."),
    sub("b.4. Slingshot shrinkage"),
    p("Estimate dE/dt and da/dt."),
    sub("b.5. Radius-halving time"),
    p("For initial radius a1 find the slingshot time to reach a1/2. Evaluate at σ=200 km/s,a1=1 pc,ρ=\\(10^{4}\\)M☉/pc³."),
    sub("c.1. Radiative shrinkage"),
    p("Find da/dt under the supplied gravitational-wave law."),
    sub("c.2. Radiative merger time"),
    p("Find the time from initial a2≫rg=2GM/c² down to a radius of order rg."),
    sub("c.3. Hubble-time radius"),
    p("For M=\\(10^{8}\\)M☉ find aH giving TGW=tH."),
    sub("d.1. Galaxy circular speed"),
    p("Find the circular speed in the density profile ρ(r)=σ²/(4πGr²)."),
    sub("d.2. Frictional orbital decay"),
    p("Use A3 with typical relative speed σ and constant lnΛ to estimate da/dt before binary formation."),
    sub("d.3. Binary formation radius"),
    fig("batch8-binary-formation", "Original black-hole motion in a merged galaxy before and after binary formation."),
    p("Estimate and calculate a1 where mutual hole gravity becomes significant."),
    sub("d.4. Initial sinking time"),
    p("Both holes initially have circular radius a0=2 kpc. Calculate time T1 to form the binary."),
    sub("d.5. Transition to radiation"),
    p("After binary formation take the stellar density fixed at ρ1=ρ(a1). Estimate the radius a2 below which gravitational waves dominate."),
    sub("d.6. Late-stage durations"),
    p("Estimate T2 from a1 to a2 by slingshot and T3 from a2 to merger by radiation."),
    sub("d.7. Total evolution time"),
    p("Estimate Tev from galaxy merging to black-hole merging."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Evolution of a supermassive black-hole binary"),
    sub("a.1. Weak scattering coefficient"),
    p("Integrate the transverse gravitational impulse along the undeflected straight path. It gives Δp⊥=2GMm/(bv), hence α=2GM/(bv²)=2b1/b and k=2."),
    sub("a.2. Longitudinal impulse"),
    p("The star keeps its speed in the hole frame. To order α², its longitudinal momentum change has magnitude mvα²/2. The hole receives drag \\(\\Delta p_x=-mv\\alpha^2/2=-2G^2M^2m/(b^2v^3)\\)."),
    sub("a.3. Dynamical friction"),
    p("The rate in an annulus db is 2πbvn db. Therefore \\[F_{DF}=-\\frac{4\\pi G^2M^2\\rho}{v^2}\\ln\\frac R{b_1}.\\] The negative sign is relative to the velocity. The logarithm comes from db/b."),
    sub("a.4. Coulomb logarithm"),
    p("b1=GM/v²≈10.8 pc, so lnΛ≈ln(20000/10.8)≈7.53."),
    sub("b.1. Circular binary energy"),
    p("Use Mvbin²/a=GM²/(4a²). Then \\(v_{bin}=\\sqrt{GM/(4a)}\\) and \\(E=2(Mv_{bin}^2/2)-GM^2/(2a)=-GM^2/(4a)\\)."),
    sub("b.2. Gravitational focusing"),
    p("Angular momentum gives bσ=rmvp. Energy gives vp²=σ²+2GM2/rm. Thus \\(b=r_m\\sqrt{1+2GM_2/(\\sigma^2r_m)}\\)."),
    sub("b.3. Collision interval"),
    p("Use M2=2M and rm≈a. Strong focusing gives bmax²≈4GMa/σ². The rate πbmax²nσ gives \\(\\Delta t\\simeq m\\sigma/(4\\pi GM\\rho a)\\). This approximate cross section sets the numerical convention used below."),
    sub("b.4. Slingshot shrinkage"),
    p("Each ejected star takes approximately mvbin²/2=GmM/(8a). Divide by the B3 interval: \\(\\dot E\\simeq-\\pi G^2M^2\\rho/(2\\sigma)\\). Since dE/da=GM²/(4a²), \\(\\dot a\\simeq-2\\pi G\\rho a^2/\\sigma\\)."),
    sub("b.5. Radius-halving time"),
    p("Integrate da/a²=−(2πGρ/σ)dt. The halving time is \\(T_{SS}=\\sigma/(2\\pi G\\rho a_1)\\simeq7.3\\times10^{-4}\\,\\mathrm{Gy}\\)."),
    sub("c.1. Radiative shrinkage"),
    p("Using ω²=GM/(4a³) and I=2Ma² yields \\(\\dot E=-64G^4M^5/(5c^5a^5)\\). Divide by dE/da=GM²/(4a²): \\(\\dot a=-256G^3M^3/(5c^5a^3)\\)."),
    sub("c.2. Radiative merger time"),
    p("Integration gives \\(T_{GW}=5c^5(a_2^4-r_g^4)/(1024G^3M^3)\\simeq5c^5a_2^4/(1024G^3M^3)\\). The approximation neglects rg⁴."),
    sub("c.3. Hubble-time radius"),
    p("\\(a_H=[1024G^3M^3t_H/(5c^5)]^{1/4}\\simeq0.098\\,\\mathrm{pc}\\)."),
    sub("d.1. Galaxy circular speed"),
    p("The enclosed stellar mass is M*(r)=σ²r/G. Therefore v²=GM*(r)/r=σ² and v=σ."),
    sub("d.2. Frictional orbital decay"),
    p("Circular kinetic energy is constant, while dU/da=Mσ²/a. The energy-loss rate is FDFσ=−GM²σlnΛ/a². Thus \\(\\dot a\\simeq-GM\\ln\\Lambda/(a\\sigma)\\)."),
    sub("d.3. Binary formation radius"),
    p("A mass-equivalence estimate M*(a1)≈M gives \\(a_1\\simeq GM/\\sigma^2\\simeq10.8\\,\\mathrm{pc}\\). Equating the actual forces instead gives GM/(4σ²)≈2.7 pc. Both are legitimate within the source's estimate criterion. Use the 10.8 pc convention consistently below."),
    sub("d.4. Initial sinking time"),
    p("Integrate D2: \\(T_1=\\sigma(a_0^2-a_1^2)/(2GM\\ln\\Lambda)\\simeq0.121\\,\\mathrm{Gy}\\). The a1² correction is negligible."),
    sub("d.5. Transition to radiation"),
    p("Equate the B4 and C1 energy-loss magnitudes. Then \\[a_2^5=\\frac{128G^2M^3\\sigma}{5\\pi c^5\\rho_1}.\\] For a1=10.8 pc, ρ1≈\\(6.3\\times10^{3}\\)M☉/pc³ and a2≈0.018 pc. The alternative 2.7 pc formation convention gives a2≈0.010 pc."),
    sub("d.6. Late-stage durations"),
    p("\\[T_2\\simeq\\frac\\sigma{2\\pi G\\rho_1}\\left(\\frac1{a_2}-\\frac1{a_1}\\right)\\simeq0.063\\,\\mathrm{Gy},\\quad T_3\\simeq\\frac{5c^5a_2^4}{1024G^3M^3}\\simeq0.016\\,\\mathrm{Gy}.\\] At the transition T3≈σ/(8πGρ1a2), one quarter of the dominant term in T2. The alternative a1 convention gives 0.0068 and 0.0017 Gy."),
    sub("d.7. Total evolution time"),
    p("Add T1+T2+T3≈0.121+0.063+0.016=0.20 Gy. The alternative force-equivalence estimate gives approximately 0.13 Gy. Both use the stated replenished stellar population approximation. Depletion of stars on intersecting orbits would invalidate a constant slingshot rate, the final-parsec issue mentioned in the source."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2018-t3"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Thermoelectric generators and refrigerators"),
    p("Retain Joule, Seebeck and Peltier effects only, with temperature-independent coefficients. A thermocouple AB has emf α(T1−T2), junction Peltier heat πI and no heat exchange with surroundings except at reservoirs. Fourier heat current is q=−kS dT/dx. Both legs have length L and parameters ρA,ρB,kA,kB,SA,SB. Their series electrical resistance is \\(R=L(\\rho_A/S_A+\\rho_B/S_B)\\) and parallel thermal conductance is \\(K=(k_AS_A+k_BS_B)/L\\). Define Z=α²/(KR). For numerical work each material has resistivity \\(1.0\\times10^{-5}\\) Ω m and conductivity 1.4 W m⁻¹ K⁻¹, L=0.020 m and α=420 μV/K. Material A is Bi2Te2.7Se0.3 and B is Bi0.5Sb1.5Te3. In generator mode T1>T2. In refrigerator mode the current removes heat from the isolated upper junction at T1 and rejects it to the lower sink at T2, eventually T1<T2. Ignore radiation and convection."),
    fig("batch8-heated-bar", "Original conducting bar with current, cross-section, coordinate and endpoint temperatures."),
    sub("a1.1. Temperature profile with Joule heating"),
    p("A homogeneous leg spans x=0 to L at fixed T1,T2 with current I. Find steady T(x)."),
    sub("a1.2. Heat current along a leg"),
    p("Find q(x),q(0),q(L)."),
    sub("a2.1. Reversible hot-side heat"),
    p("Neglect resistivity. Find heat received by the electron gas at the hot junction with coefficient π1."),
    sub("a2.2. Reversible cold-side heat"),
    p("Find heat delivered by the electron gas to the cold junction with coefficient π2."),
    sub("a2.3. Reversible electrical power"),
    p("Find the output power using α."),
    sub("a2.4. Kelvin relation"),
    p("Using the ideal reversible thermodynamic cycle, relate π to α and junction temperature T."),
    sub("a3.1. Generator heat balances"),
    fig("batch8-thermoelectric-generator", "Original two thermoelectric legs between hot and cold reservoirs feeding load RL."),
    p("Include Joule heating. Find hot-side input q1 and cold-side output q2."),
    sub("a3.2. Efficiency with a load"),
    p("Let m=RL/R. Find η in terms of α,K,R,T1,T2,m."),
    sub("a3.3. Figure-of-merit form"),
    p("Write η using Z,ηc=(T1−T2)/T1,T1 and m."),
    sub("a4.1. Efficiency at maximum power"),
    p("Find ηP as a function of Z,T1,T2."),
    sub("a4.2. Load for maximum efficiency"),
    p("Find M, the resistance ratio maximizing η."),
    sub("a4.3. Maximum generator efficiency"),
    p("Express ηmax through T1,T2,Z and M."),
    sub("a5.1. Optimal leg-area ratio"),
    p("Find SB/SA maximizing Z for the two materials."),
    sub("a5.2. Best material figure of merit"),
    p("Find Zm."),
    sub("a6.1. Numerical efficiency at maximum power"),
    p("At T1=423 K,T2=303 K calculate ηopt when both load power and material figure of merit are maximized. Compare Carnot."),
    sub("a6.2. Numerical maximum efficiency"),
    p("Calculate ηmax for the same materials and temperatures."),
    sub("b1.1. Cooling power"),
    fig("batch8-thermoelectric-cooler", "Original two thermoelectric legs and driven current cooling an isolated upper junction."),
    p("Find qC in terms of α,K,R,T1,T2,I."),
    sub("b1.2. Maximum temperature depression"),
    p("Find ΔTmax=T2−T1min using Z and T1min."),
    sub("b2.1. Minimum cold temperature"),
    p("For T2=300 K and the best material figure of merit find T1min."),
    sub("b2.2. Working current at the limit"),
    p("Take SA=SB=\\(10^{-4}\\) m² and calculate Iw at that minimum temperature."),
    sub("b3.1. Coefficient of performance"),
    p("For a temperature difference below its maximum, find β=qC/P."),
    sub("b3.2. Current for maximum COP"),
    p("Find Iβ using α,Z,R,T1,T2."),
    sub("b3.3. Maximum COP"),
    p("Find βmax."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Thermoelectric generators and refrigerators"),
    sub("a1.1. Temperature profile with Joule heating"),
    p("Heat balance gives \\(kS T^{\\prime\\prime}=-\\rho I^2/S\\). Integrate and impose both temperatures: \\[T(x)=T_1+(T_2-T_1)x/L+\\frac{\\rho I^2}{2kS^2}x(L-x).\\]"),
    sub("a1.2. Heat current along a leg"),
    p("Differentiate A1.1: \\(q=kS(T_1-T_2)/L+(\\rho I^2/S)(x-L/2)\\). With R=ρL/S and K=kS/L, \\(q(0)=K(T_1-T_2)-RI^2/2\\) and \\(q(L)=K(T_1-T_2)+RI^2/2\\). Half the Joule heat flows to each end."),
    sub("a2.1. Reversible hot-side heat"),
    p("By the Peltier definition, \\(q_1=\\pi_1I\\)."),
    sub("a2.2. Reversible cold-side heat"),
    p("\\(q_2=\\pi_2I\\), with the positive quantities denoting received and rejected heat in the generator cycle."),
    sub("a2.3. Reversible electrical power"),
    p("\\(P=\\mathcal E I=\\alpha(T_1-T_2)I\\)."),
    sub("a2.4. Kelvin relation"),
    p("The ideal efficiency is P/q1=(T1−T2)/T1. Substitute the preceding heat and power to obtain π1=αT1. At any junction \\(\\pi=\\alpha T\\)."),
    sub("a3.1. Generator heat balances"),
    p("Add the conductive, Peltier and split Joule contributions: \\[q_1=K(T_1-T_2)+\\alpha T_1I-RI^2/2,\\quad q_2=K(T_1-T_2)+\\alpha T_2I+RI^2/2.\\] Their difference is load power α(T1−T2)I−RI²."),
    sub("a3.2. Efficiency with a load"),
    p("The circuit current is \\(I=\\alpha(T_1-T_2)/[R(1+m)]\\). Divide I²RL by q1 to get \\[\\eta=\\frac{m(T_1-T_2)}{(KR/\\alpha^2)(1+m)^2+T_1(1+m)-(T_1-T_2)/2}.\\]"),
    sub("a3.3. Figure-of-merit form"),
    p("Substitute Z=α²/(KR): \\[\\eta=\\eta_c\\frac m{(1+m)^2/(ZT_1)+(1+m)-\\eta_c/2}.\\]"),
    sub("a4.1. Efficiency at maximum power"),
    p("Power is proportional to m/(1+m)² and peaks at m=1. Substitution gives \\[\\eta_P=\\frac{T_1-T_2}{4/Z+(3T_1+T_2)/2}.\\] The source prints T1−T1 in one numerator, a typographical error corrected by its derivation."),
    sub("a4.2. Load for maximum efficiency"),
    p("Differentiate A3.3 with respect to m. The positive stationary root is \\(M=\\sqrt{1+Z(T_1+T_2)/2}\\). It is larger than one for Z>0."),
    sub("a4.3. Maximum generator efficiency"),
    p("At m=M the efficiency simplifies to \\[\\eta_{max}=\\frac{T_1-T_2}{T_1}\\frac{M-1}{M+T_2/T_1},\\qquad M=\\sqrt{1+Z(T_1+T_2)/2}.\\]"),
    sub("a5.1. Optimal leg-area ratio"),
    p("Minimize KR=(kASA+kBSB)(ρA/SA+ρB/SB). Setting the derivative with respect to SB/SA to zero gives \\(S_B/S_A=\\sqrt{\\rho_Bk_A/(\\rho_Ak_B)}\\)."),
    sub("a5.2. Best material figure of merit"),
    p("The minimum product is \\(KR=(\\sqrt{\\rho_Ak_A}+\\sqrt{\\rho_Bk_B})^2\\), hence \\[Z_m=\\frac{\\alpha^2}{(\\sqrt{\\rho_Ak_A}+\\sqrt{\\rho_Bk_B})^2}.\\]"),
    sub("a6.1. Numerical efficiency at maximum power"),
    p("The matched materials give Zm=α²/(4ρk)=0.00315 K⁻¹. With m=1, ηopt≈0.0586, or 5.86%. Using rounded Z=0.0032 gives the source's approximately 5.84% to 5.9%. Carnot is 120/423≈28.4%, so ηopt/ηc≈0.21."),
    sub("a6.2. Numerical maximum efficiency"),
    p("M=√(1+0.00315×363)≈1.46. The maximum efficiency is approximately 0.060, or 6.0%. It uses a different load from maximum power."),
    sub("b1.1. Cooling power"),
    p("The upper Peltier term removes heat while conduction from the warm sink and half the Joule heat oppose cooling: \\[q_C=\\alpha T_1I-K(T_2-T_1)-RI^2/2.\\]"),
    sub("b1.2. Maximum temperature depression"),
    p("At fixed temperatures qC is largest at I=αT1/R. Its zero at the minimum attainable temperature gives \\(\\Delta T_{max}=ZT_{1,min}^2/2\\)."),
    sub("b2.1. Minimum cold temperature"),
    p("The positive solution of ZmT1²/2+T1−T2=0 is \\(T_{1,min}=[\\sqrt{1+2Z_mT_2}-1]/Z_m\\simeq222\\,\\mathrm K\\)."),
    sub("b2.2. Working current at the limit"),
    p("The pair resistance is R=2ρL/S=0.004 Ω. Thus \\(I_w=\\alpha T_{1,min}/R\\simeq23.3\\,\\mathrm A\\)."),
    sub("b3.1. Coefficient of performance"),
    p("The required source power is \\(P=\\alpha(T_2-T_1)I+RI^2\\). Therefore \\[\\beta=\\frac{\\alpha T_1I-K(T_2-T_1)-RI^2/2}{\\alpha(T_2-T_1)I+RI^2}.\\]"),
    sub("b3.2. Current for maximum COP"),
    p("Let TM=(T1+T2)/2. Differentiate β and take the positive root: \\[I_\\beta=\\frac{\\alpha(T_2-T_1)}{R(\\sqrt{1+ZT_M}-1)}.\\] This differs from the maximum-cooling-power current."),
    sub("b3.3. Maximum COP"),
    p("With \\(s=\\sqrt{1+ZT_M}\\), substitution gives \\[\\beta_{max}=\\frac{T_1}{T_2-T_1}\\frac{s-T_2/T_1}{s+1}.\\] Positive cooling at the optimum requires s>T2/T1. This tends to the Carnot COP as Z tends to infinity."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2021-t1"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Water-hammer effect"),
    p("A nonviscous liquid flows one-dimensionally in a rigid pipe of length L and radius R. A reservoir feeds its upstream end B at depth h below the free surface, and a valve at downstream end A opens to atmosphere Pa. Water has reference density ρ0=1000 kg/m³ and bulk modulus B=2.2 GPa, defined for the finite element by \\(\\Delta P=-B\\Delta V/V_0\\). Initially flow velocity is v0 in +x with pressure P0. A pressure front moves upstream at positive laboratory speed c. Valve closure changes downstream fluid velocity to v1≤0 in A. The short valve has opening radius r, cone angle β, and a contracted atmospheric jet of radius rc=r√Cc. Its inlet and contracted-jet streamlines are parallel. Contraction data (r/R,Cc at 45°,Cc at 90°) are (0,.746,.611),(.2,.747,.616),(.3,.748,.622),(.4,.749,.631),(1,1,1). For C and D treat c and ρ0 as constants, neglect valve length, use β=90° and use the incompressible steady-valve relation locally. In D additionally approximate reservoir pressure Ph by P0, as requested for the wave-reflection model, without resetting the given initial flow to zero."),
    fig("batch8-water-hammer-pipe", "Original reservoir depth, pipe length, initial flow and downstream valve."),
    sub("a.1. Wave pressure and speed"),
    p("Find α,β,γ in ΔPs=αρ0c(v1−v0) and c=β+√(γB/ρ0). State the small-flow-speed limit."),
    sub("a.2. Sudden-closure magnitude"),
    p("Calculate c and ΔPs for v0=4.0 m/s and v1=0."),
    sub("b.1. Valve pressure relation"),
    fig("batch8-water-hammer-valve", "Original conical valve, opening, inlet and contracted jet radii."),
    p("Find ΔPin=Pin−Pa in terms of ρ0,vin,r,R,Cc."),
    sub("c.1. Fully open initial flow"),
    p("Find P0 and v0 in terms of ρ0,g,h,Pa."),
    sub("c.2. First round trip after closure"),
    p("At t=0 close the valve instantly. Let τ=2L/c and Ph=P0+ρ0gh. Find pipe pressure and velocity just before t=τ/2 and just before t=τ, explaining the reflected wave."),
    sub("d.1. Finite-step closure recursion"),
    p("At times 0,τ,2τ,3τ set rn/R to .4,.3,.2,0 respectively. Let ΔPn=Pn−P0, v4=0 and ΔP0=0. Enforcing Ph≈P0, derive ΔPn/(ρ0c) from the previous step and an equation for vn for n=1,2,3."),
    sub("d.2. Graphical pressure-velocity construction"),
    p("For v0=4.0 m/s plot ΔP against X=ρ0cv in MPa. Draw the straight lines and valve curves for all four steps, label their intersections, and estimate (Xn,ΔPn). You may use c≈1500 m/s."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Water-hammer effect"),
    sub("a.1. Wave pressure and speed"),
    p("In the front frame continuity gives ρ0(c+v0)=ρ1(c+v1). Momentum gives ΔPs=−ρ0(c+v0)(v1−v0). The finite-volume modulus gives ΔPs=B(ρ1−ρ0)/ρ1. Combining yields c+v0=√(B/ρ0). Thus \\(\\alpha=-(1+v_0/c),\\ \\beta=-v_0,\\ \\gamma=1\\). For v0≪c, c≈√(B/ρ0) and ΔPs≈−ρ0cΔv. A density-based linearized modulus changes higher-order terms, as discussed in the source."),
    sub("a.2. Sudden-closure magnitude"),
    p("The acoustic speed relative to water is √(B/ρ0)=1483 m/s, and the upstream laboratory front speed is 1479 m/s. At source precision c≈\\(1.5\\times10^{3}\\) m/s. The pressure rise is v0√(ρ0B)≈5.93 MPa. The field checks the pressure in MPa."),
    sub("b.1. Valve pressure relation"),
    p("Continuity gives \\(v_c=v_{in}R^2/(C_cr^2)\\). Bernoulli between the parallel sections gives \\[\\Delta P_{in}=\\frac{\\rho_0v_{in}^2}{2}\\left[\\frac1{C_c^2}\\left(\\frac Rr\\right)^4-1\\right].\\] This applies for nonzero opening, not at the fully closed limit with zero flow."),
    sub("c.1. Fully open initial flow"),
    p("At r=R, Cc=1 so inlet and outlet speeds agree and P0=Pa. Bernoulli from the reservoir free surface gives \\(v_0=\\sqrt{2gh}\\)."),
    sub("c.2. First round trip after closure"),
    p("Just before τ/2 the compression front has nearly reached B, leaving virtually the whole pipe at \\(P=P_0+\\rho_0cv_0\\), v=0. The fixed-pressure reservoir reflects an expansion of amplitude Ph−P1. Just before τ the return front has nearly reached A, leaving \\(P=P_h\\) and \\(v=-v_0+gh/c\\). The infinitesimal regions ahead of each front retain the previous state. At the exact closed boundary velocity remains zero until the incoming front reflects, so the stated limits refer to the pipe interior."),
    sub("d.1. Finite-step closure recursion"),
    p("After each reservoir reflection, the arriving valve-side velocity is \\(v_{n-1}-\\Delta P_{n-1}/(\\rho_0c)\\). The next upstream wave therefore gives \\[\\frac{\\Delta P_n}{\\rho_0c}=v_{n-1}-v_n-\\frac{\\Delta P_{n-1}}{\\rho_0c}.\\] For n<4 set \\(k_n=(R/r_n)^4/C_n^2-1\\) and use ΔPn=ρ0knvn²/2. Thus \\[\\frac{k_n}{2c}v_n^2+v_n+\\frac{\\Delta P_{n-1}}{\\rho_0c}-v_{n-1}=0.\\] Choose the nonnegative root. For n=4 use v4=0 directly in the recursion."),
    sub("d.2. Graphical pressure-velocity construction"),
    p("Each step line is \\(\\Delta P=-X+X_{n-1}-\\Delta P_{n-1}\\). The valve parabola is \\(\\Delta P=k_nX^2/(2\\rho_0c^2)\\), with kn≈97.1,318,1646 for the first three openings. Starting at (6,0) MPa, intersections are approximately (5.37,0.624), (3.75,0.997), (1.695,1.06) and (0,0.643) MPa. The fourth curve is the vertical line X=0. Slow staged closure keeps these peaks well below the 6 MPa sudden-closure estimate. The source's first graphical table contains a mistyped 5.8 MPa coordinate, corrected by its appendix to 5.37 MPa."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2021-t2"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Ray tracing and generation of entangled light"),
    p("For a plane wave in a nonmagnetic dielectric use \\(\\mathbf B=\\mu_0\\mathbf H\\), \\(\\mathbf k\\times\\mathbf E=\\omega\\mathbf B\\), \\(\\mathbf k\\times\\mathbf H=-\\omega\\mathbf D\\), energy density \\(u=(\\mathbf E\\cdot\\mathbf D+\\mathbf H\\cdot\\mathbf B)/2\\) and Poynting vector S=E×H. Ray speed is |S|/u. In A take D=εE. In B the optic axis is z and D=(εEx,εEy,ε′Ez), with no=c√(μ0ε), ne=c√(μ0ε′). Initially k=k(sinθ,0,cosθ). A signed angle from E to D, or from S to k, is positive clockwise in the source xz view. For refraction the interface is yz, incident medium isotropic with index n, and the crystal axes x2,z2 are rotated by φ as shown. In C a nonzero quadratic polarization \\(P_i^{NL}=\\sum_{jk}\\chi^{(2)}_{ijk}E_jE_k\\) couples three waves. Positive frequencies satisfy ω≥ω2 and ω1≥ω2. Use photon energy ℏω and momentum ℏk. The specified entangled pair is \\((|x'_ay'_b\\rangle+|y'_ax'_b\\rangle)/\\sqrt2\\). Linear polarizers at a,b have axes at α,β measured from x′, and perpendicular settings add π/2."),
    sub("a.1. Isotropic phase speed"),
    p("Find vp in terms of ε and μ0."),
    sub("a.2. Isotropic index"),
    p("Find n."),
    sub("a.3. Isotropic ray"),
    p("Find the energy-flow direction and ray speed."),
    sub("b.1. Uniaxial eigenmodes"),
    p("Find all permissible D and B directions and indices for k in xz. Identify directions with only one index."),
    sub("b.2. Polarizations and walk-off angle"),
    p("Find normalized E directions, identify ordinary and extraordinary waves, and give tanα."),
    sub("b.3. Arbitrary propagation azimuth"),
    p("Extend the modes to arbitrary k̂ making angle θ with z."),
    sub("b.4. Extraordinary energy flow"),
    p("Find tanαr,vr,Ŝ for both modes and express ray index ns=c/vr through Ŝ."),
    sub("b.5. Fermat refraction quadratic"),
    fig("batch8-anisotropic-interface", "Original incident and refracted rays with rotated optical axes at an interface."),
    p("For extraordinary refraction derive A tan²θ2+B̄tanθ2+C=0. Define P1=no²cos²φ+ne²sin²φ, P2=no²sin²φ+ne²cos²φ and P3=(no²−ne²)sinφcosφ. Find the coefficients and tanθ2 at φ=0,π/2."),
    sub("c.1. Phase matching"),
    p("Find frequency and wave-vector matching relations generated by the quadratic polarization. Interpret them as photon conservation laws and state the decay case."),
    sub("c.2. Forbidden collinear channels"),
    p("If no and ne each increase with frequency, which of o→o+o,o→e+o,o→o+e,o→e+e,e→o+o,e→e+o,e→o+e,e→e+e are impossible for collinear forward waves?"),
    sub("c.3. Down-conversion cones"),
    fig("batch8-downconversion-cones", "Original optic-axis geometry and intersecting ordinary and extraordinary photon cones."),
    p("Take ne<no and a collinearly phase-matched pump along z′ with Ωp=Ωe+Ωo and Kp=Ke+Ko. Its optic axis is in x′z′ at θ<π/2 to z′. For near-degenerate non-collinear daughters ω1=ω2=Ω, use q=k2⊥=−k1⊥. Let \\(N_e=(1/n_e)\\partial n_e/\\partial\\theta\\) at (Ωe,θ), ue=∂ω1/∂k1 and uo=dω2/dk2. In the source's paraxial expansion the q locus is M(qx′+N)²+Mqy′²=L. Find M,N,L and the cone-axis tilt and half-angle. Retain the source expansion's linear angular-index variation and its second-order geometric corrections."),
    sub("c.4. Coincidence probabilities"),
    fig("batch8-entangled-polarizers", "Original two polarizer orientations for coincident photon detection."),
    p("For the supplied symmetric entangled pair calculate P(α,β),P(α,β⊥),P(α⊥,β),P(α⊥,β⊥)."),
    sub("c.5. CHSH violation"),
    p("Assign outcomes ±1 to each polarizer's orthogonal outputs. Find E(α,β) and S=|E(α,β)−E(α,β′)|+|E(α′,β)+E(α′,β′)|. Evaluate at α=π/4,α′=0,β=−π/8,β′=π/8 and compare to the classical bound 2."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Ray tracing and generation of entangled light"),
    sub("a.1. Isotropic phase speed"),
    p("Eliminate B,H from Maxwell equations. With k·E=0, k²E=ω²μ0εE. Thus \\(v_p=\\omega/k=1/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\epsilon}\\)."),
    sub("a.2. Isotropic index"),
    p("By n=c/vp, \\(n=c\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\epsilon}\\)."),
    sub("a.3. Isotropic ray"),
    p("E,H,k are mutually orthogonal and E×H points along k. The electric and magnetic energy densities are equal, so u=|S|/vp. Thus Ŝ=k̂ and vr=vp=1/√(μ0ε)."),
    sub("b.1. Uniaxial eigenmodes"),
    p("The ordinary mode has D̂=±ŷ, B̂=±(−cosθ,0,sinθ) and n=no. The extraordinary mode has D̂=±(cosθ,0,−sinθ), B̂=±ŷ with correlated signs and \\[n(\\theta)=\\frac{n_on_e}{\\sqrt{n_o^2\\sin^2\\theta+n_e^2\\cos^2\\theta}}.\\] At θ=0 or π both indices equal no. For a genuinely anisotropic crystal these are the optic-axis directions."),
    sub("b.2. Polarizations and walk-off angle"),
    p("The ordinary mode has Ê=±ŷ and α=0. The extraordinary direction is \\[\\hat E=\\pm\\frac{(n_e^2\\cos\\theta,0,-n_o^2\\sin\\theta)}{\\sqrt{n_e^4\\cos^2\\theta+n_o^4\\sin^2\\theta}}.\\] Comparing slopes of E and D gives \\[\\tan\\alpha=\\frac{(n_o^2-n_e^2)\\tan\\theta}{n_e^2+n_o^2\\tan^2\\theta}.\\] Overall sign of polarization is immaterial."),
    sub("b.3. Arbitrary propagation azimuth"),
    p("Let \\(\\hat t=(\\hat k-\\cos\\theta\\hat z)/\\sin\\theta\\). The same two indices apply. Ordinary Ê is ±(k̂×ẑ)/sinθ. Extraordinary Ê is proportional to \\(n_e^2\\cos\\theta\\hat t-n_o^2\\sin\\theta\\hat z\\), normalized as in B2. At sinθ=0 choose any two transverse orthogonal polarizations, since the modes are degenerate. This vector form avoids the inconsistent expanded z coefficient printed in one source formula."),
    sub("b.4. Extraordinary energy flow"),
    p("Ordinary light has αr=0, vr=c/no and Ŝ=k̂. For extraordinary light αr=α, and with \\(D=n_e^2\\cos^2\\theta+n_o^2\\sin^2\\theta\\), \\(W=\\sqrt{n_e^4\\cos^2\\theta+n_o^4\\sin^2\\theta}\\), \\[\\hat S=(n_o^2\\sin\\theta,0,n_e^2\\cos\\theta)/W,\\qquad v_r=\\frac{cW}{n_on_e\\sqrt D}=v_p/\\cos\\alpha_r.\\] The extraordinary ray index is \\(n_s=\\sqrt{n_e^2(\\hat S\\cdot\\hat x)^2+n_o^2(\\hat S\\cdot\\hat z)^2}\\). For ordinary light ns=no."),
    sub("b.5. Fermat refraction quadratic"),
    p("Write s=n sinθ1. Differentiating optical travel time at the interface gives \\(s^2=(P_3-P_1\\tan\\theta_2)^2/(P_1\\tan^2\\theta_2-2P_3\\tan\\theta_2+P_2)\\). Therefore \\[A=P_1(s^2-P_1),\\quad\\bar B=-2P_3(s^2-P_1),\\quad C=P_2s^2-P_3^2.\\] Select the root consistent with the unsquared Fermat equation and forward energy flow. At φ=0, \\(\\tan\\theta_2=n_es/[n_o\\sqrt{n_o^2-s^2}]\\). At φ=π/2, \\(\\tan\\theta_2=n_os/[n_e\\sqrt{n_e^2-s^2}]\\)."),
    sub("c.1. Phase matching"),
    p("Products of plane-wave phases yield sum and difference components. Matching gives \\(\\omega=\\omega_1\\pm\\omega_2\\), \\(\\mathbf k=\\mathbf k_1\\pm\\mathbf k_2\\), with consistent signs. Multiplication by ℏ gives energy and momentum conservation. Decay of the pump photon uses \\(\\omega=\\omega_1+\\omega_2\\) and \\(\\mathbf k=\\mathbf k_1+\\mathbf k_2\\)."),
    sub("c.2. Forbidden collinear channels"),
    p("The same-polarization channels o→o+o and e→e+e are impossible. Their parent index at the larger frequency exceeds either daughter index, while momentum matching would require a frequency-weighted average of the daughter indices. No cross-polarization channel is universally excluded without additional information relating no and ne."),
    sub("c.3. Down-conversion cones"),
    p("Put ν=Ω−Ωe, so Ω−Ωo=−ν. The ordinary longitudinal component is \\(k_{2z}=K_o-\\nu/u_o-q^2/(2K_o)\\). The source angular approximation gives \\(k_{1z}=K_e+\\nu/u_e+N_eq_{x'}+[N_e\\cot\\theta-1]q^2/(2K_e)\\). Sum these and impose Kp=Ke+Ko. Completing the square gives \\[M=\\frac{K_o(1-N_e\\cot\\theta)+K_e}{2K_eK_o},\\quad N=-\\frac{N_e}{2M},\\quad L=-\\nu(1/u_o-1/u_e)+\\frac{N_e^2}{4M}.\\] The centre is qx′=−N. Its tilt magnitude is |N|/Ko and cone half-angle is √(L/M)/Ko, provided L≥0. Signs describe the chosen x′ direction. The source changes coefficient letters mid-derivation and has some dimensional typos in its final angle formula. These expressions follow its stated longitudinal expansion. A general expansion retaining curvature of ne(θ) requires further second angular derivatives."),
    sub("c.4. Coincidence probabilities"),
    p("The joint transmission amplitude is (cosαsinβ+sinαcosβ)/√2. Squaring gives \\[P(\\alpha,\\beta)=P(\\alpha_\\perp,\\beta_\\perp)=\\tfrac12\\sin^2(\\alpha+\\beta),\\] \\[P(\\alpha,\\beta_\\perp)=P(\\alpha_\\perp,\\beta)=\\tfrac12\\cos^2(\\alpha+\\beta).\\] The field checks P(α,β)."),
    sub("c.5. CHSH violation"),
    p("The four normalized joint probabilities give \\(E=-\\cos[2(\\alpha+\\beta)]\\). Therefore S is the indicated absolute-value combination of these cosines with sums of angles. At the specified settings S=2√2>2. The source incorrectly simplifies its own C4 probabilities to a difference of angles in one line, but both the consistent sum convention and a reversed b-axis convention give the same S for these settings. The field checks the numerical S."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2021-t3"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Magnetic levitation"),
    p("A small spherical permanent magnet can be treated as a point dipole m=q_mδm, represented by south and north magnetic charges −qm,+qm with displacement δm from south to north. The north pole defines its position. A monopole produces \\(\\mathbf B=\\mu_0q_m\\hat r/(4\\pi r^2)\\) and experiences force qmB. Write B=−∇Φ. A uniform conducting film extends infinitely in xy, occupies −d≤z≤0, has conductivity σ, and lies height h below the source, with h≫d. Neglect displacement current and take induced volume current uniform across d. In A a north monopole appears at (0,0,h) at t=0 and remains there. Neglect propagation delay. Initially its image above the film calculation is +qm at (0,0,−h), and below-film calculation uses −qm at the real source position. The supplied film equation is ∂²B′z/∂z²=μ0σ∂B′z/∂t after neglecting lateral derivatives in the thin-film approximation. In B a source moves along +x at fixed h for its whole prior history and is at x=0 now. In C take a type-I superconductor with σ→∞ and a horizontally fixed dipole m x̂ of mass M0 in gravity −g ẑ. Ignore orientation coupling and air damping."),
    fig("batch8-monopole-film", "Original magnetic monopole above a thin conducting film with height h and thickness d."),
    sub("a.1. Initial field above the film"),
    p("Find total B(ρ,z) at t=0 for z≥0 away from the monopole."),
    sub("a.2. Initial field below the film"),
    p("Find the initial total field for z≤−d."),
    sub("a.3. Initial surface flux"),
    p("Find initial magnetic flux through either film surface."),
    sub("a.4. Induced current density"),
    p("Find the initial induced volume current j(ρ) using the thin-film approximation."),
    sub("a.5. First-order surface evolution"),
    p("Integrate the film equation across thickness to obtain a first-order equation near z≈0."),
    sub("a.6. Surface evolution solution"),
    p("Find the general form of B′z near the surface for t>0."),
    sub("a.7. Receding image speed"),
    p("Show the induced field represents a downward-moving image and find its speed."),
    sub("b.1. Hopping-source images"),
    p("Approximate motion by hops vτ at times −nτ,n≥0. Each hop creates +qm at the new position and −qm at the old. Give present image positions for both types."),
    sub("b.2. Image monopole potential"),
    p("Write and evaluate the image-potential sum Φ+(x,z) in the y=0 plane as τ tends to zero."),
    sub("b.3. Lift and drag on a vertical dipole"),
    fig("batch8-moving-dipole-film", "Original vertical dipole travelling horizontally above the conducting film."),
    p("The moving dipole has fixed moment m ẑ. Find its force at the present position (0,0,h)."),
    sub("b.4. Copper image speed"),
    p("For σ=\\(5.9\\times10^{7}\\) Ω⁻¹m⁻¹,d=0.50 cm,h=5.0 cm calculate v0."),
    sub("b.5. Skin-depth correction"),
    p("The skin depth is δ=√[2/(ωμ0σ)] with ω=max(v,v0)/h. Estimate v0(v) in low- and high-speed regimes, replacing film thickness by skin depth if smaller."),
    sub("b.6. Crossover speed"),
    p("Find vc where the low- and high-speed estimates meet."),
    sub("c.1. Superconducting equilibrium height"),
    p("Find h0 for the horizontal dipole of mass M0."),
    sub("c.2. Vertical oscillation frequency"),
    p("Find angular frequency Ω of small vertical oscillations."),
    sub("c.3. Micromagnet height"),
    p("A sphere has R=1.0 μm,ρ0=7400 kg/m³,magnetization M=0.75 T/μ0 and g=9.8 m/s². Calculate h0."),
    sub("c.4. Micromagnet oscillations"),
    p("Calculate Ω for that sphere."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Magnetic levitation"),
    sub("a.1. Initial field above the film"),
    p("Sum the real and positive image fields: \\[\\mathbf B=\\frac{\\mu_0q_m}{4\\pi}\\left[\\frac{\\boldsymbol\\rho+(z-h)\\hat z}{(\\rho^2+(z-h)^2)^{3/2}}+\\frac{\\boldsymbol\\rho+(z+h)\\hat z}{(\\rho^2+(z+h)^2)^{3/2}}\\right].\\]"),
    sub("a.2. Initial field below the film"),
    p("The negative image at the source cancels its field in the lower region, so B=0."),
    sub("a.3. Initial surface flux"),
    p("At z=0 the two normal components cancel pointwise, and below the film the field vanishes. The total flux through any patch of either surface is zero. The source writes B′z=0 in one explanation, but it is the total Bz that vanishes, not the image contribution by itself."),
    sub("a.4. Induced current density"),
    p("Ampère's law across the film gives \\(\\mathbf j=(\\hat z\\times\\mathbf B_{above})/(\\mu_0d)\\). Thus \\[\\mathbf j=\\frac{q_m}{2\\pi d}\\frac{\\hat z\\times\\boldsymbol\\rho}{(h^2+\\rho^2)^{3/2}}.\\] The current is azimuthal and produces the opposing initial normal field."),
    sub("a.5. First-order surface evolution"),
    p("The induced normal field is even about the midplane, so its derivatives at opposite faces are opposite. Neglect variation of its time derivative across d. Integration gives \\(2\\partial_zB_z^{\\prime}=\\mu_0\\sigma d\\,\\partial_tB_z^{\\prime}\\), or \\(\\partial_tB_z^{\\prime}=v_0\\partial_zB_z^{\\prime}\\), with v0=2/(μ0σd)."),
    sub("a.6. Surface evolution solution"),
    p("The characteristic solution is \\(B_z^{\\prime}(\\rho,z;t)=f(\\rho,z+v_0t)\\), with f determined by the initial induced field."),
    sub("a.7. Receding image speed"),
    p("The initial image field depends on z+h. Replacing z by z+v0t places the image at zimage=−h−v0t. Thus the downward speed is \\(v_0=2/(\\mu_0\\sigma d)\\)."),
    sub("b.1. Hopping-source images"),
    p("Positive images are at \\((-nv\\tau,0,-h-nv_0\\tau)\\). Negative images are at \\((-(n+1)v\\tau,0,-h-nv_0\\tau)\\). Their differing lateral positions retain the history of motion."),
    sub("b.2. Image monopole potential"),
    p("Put H=z+h. The sum is \\[\\Phi_+=\\frac{\\mu_0q_m}{4\\pi}\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\left[\\frac1{\\sqrt{(x+nv\\tau)^2+(H+nv_0\\tau)^2}}-\\frac1{\\sqrt{(x+(n+1)v\\tau)^2+(H+nv_0\\tau)^2}}\\right].\\] Its continuum limit is \\(\\mu_0q_mv/(4\\pi)\\int_0^\\infty(x+vt)[(x+vt)^2+(H+v_0t)^2]^{-3/2}dt\\). Evaluation gives \\[\\Phi_+=\\frac{\\mu_0q_mv}{4\\pi(Hv-v_0x)}\\left[\\frac H{\\sqrt{x^2+H^2}}-\\frac{v_0}{\\sqrt{v^2+v_0^2}}\\right].\\] At a removable zero of the denominator use the integral or its continuous limit."),
    sub("b.3. Lift and drag on a vertical dipole"),
    p("Form the image-dipole potential \\(\\Phi_T=\\delta_m\\partial_z\\Phi_+\\), differentiate for the field, then take the north-minus-south force while holding the image source fixed during field differentiation. This yields \\[F_z=\\frac{3\\mu_0m^2}{32\\pi h^4}\\left(1-\\frac{v_0}{\\sqrt{v^2+v_0^2}}\\right),\\quad F_x=-\\frac{v_0}{v}F_z,\\quad F_y=0.\\] Lift is positive and drag negative for v>0. Both vanish continuously as v tends to zero."),
    sub("b.4. Copper image speed"),
    p("\\(v_0=2/(\\mu_0\\sigma d)\\simeq5.4\\,\\mathrm{m/s}\\)."),
    sub("b.5. Skin-depth correction"),
    p("At low v the skin depth exceeds d, so v0(v)=2/(μ0σd), the B4 constant. At high v, δ<d and ω≈v/h, so \\[v_0(v)=\\frac2{\\mu_0\\sigma\\delta}=\\sqrt{\\frac{2v}{\\mu_0\\sigma h}}=\\sqrt{v_0dv/h}.\\] This is an effective-thickness estimate beyond the uniform-through-thickness low-speed model."),
    sub("b.6. Crossover speed"),
    p("Set δ=d with ω=vc/h: \\(v_c=2h/(\\mu_0\\sigma d^2)=v_0h/d\\). For the copper dimensions it is about 54 m/s."),
    sub("c.1. Superconducting equilibrium height"),
    p("For a perfect diamagnetic plane the horizontal image has the same horizontal moment at z=−h. Its repulsive force is \\(F_z=3\\mu_0m^2/(64\\pi h^4)\\). Balance M0g to obtain \\[h_0=[3\\mu_0m^2/(64\\pi M_0g)]^{1/4}.\\]"),
    sub("c.2. Vertical oscillation frequency"),
    p("The derivative of the magnetic force at h0 is −4M0g/h0. Thus \\(\\Omega=\\sqrt{4g/h_0}\\). This is angular frequency in rad/s."),
    sub("c.3. Micromagnet height"),
    p("Use m=(4πR³/3)M and M0=(4πR³/3)ρ0. Then \\(h_0=[\\mu_0M^2R^3/(16\\rho_0g)]^{1/4}\\simeq25\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}\\)."),
    sub("c.4. Micromagnet oscillations"),
    p("Using h0≈25 μm, \\(\\Omega\\simeq1.25\\times10^3\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}\\), or f≈200 Hz. The source loosely labels the angular frequency in kHz and substitutes a rounded 30 μm in one line. Angular frequency and cycles per second are distinguished here."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2022-t1"] = {
  statement: [
    h("The Stern-Gerlach experiment"),
    p("Silver atoms of mass m=\\(1.80\\times10^{-25}\\) kg leave an oven at T=1200 K with a common beam velocity along +z. Ignore gravity. They travel l1=0.25 m before an inhomogeneous magnetic region of length l2=0.50 m, then another l3=0.25 m to a screen. Each atom has moment ±μs x̂. For the field model two infinite insulated wires lie at (0,−a,z) and (0,+a,z), carrying −I0 ẑ and +I0 ẑ respectively in a medium of permeability μ=μ0μr. A narrow gap between two circles centred at C=(xc,0) and D=(xd,0), each passing through both wires, is replaced by low-pressure air. Assume its magnetic field is unchanged by removing the magnetic material. Along +x the right intersections of the C and D circles are R and Q, and P is the midpoint RQ. For numerical work use μr=\\(10^{4}\\),a=OC=0.60 cm,OD=0.80 cm,I0=2.00 A and μ0=\\(4\\pi\\times10^{-7}\\) SI. Use kB=\\(1.381\\times10^{-23}\\) J/K."),
    fig("batch8-stern-gerlach-beam", "Original oven, silver beam, magnet length and two resolved screen spots."),
    sub("a.1. Thermal beam speed"),
    p("Calculate vz≈√(3kBT/m)."),
    sub("b.1. Beam splitting"),
    p("Derive the screen separation Δx using a constant field gradient Gx=dB/dx."),
    sub("c.1. Two-wire magnetic field"),
    fig("batch8-stern-gerlach-poles", "Original opposite wire currents, circular magnetic pole boundaries and the narrow air gap."),
    p("Find vector B(x,y,0) in the magnetic medium."),
    sub("c.2. Field direction on a circular boundary"),
    p("On the circle centred at (xc,0) of radius √(xc²+a²), give the field directions at rightmost R and uppermost P0."),
    sub("c.3. Air-gap axial field"),
    p("Using the supplied continuity assumption, state B(x,0) in the gap."),
    sub("d.1. Dipole force magnitude"),
    p("Find the x-force magnitude for a silver atom in this axial field."),
    sub("e.1. Field and gradient at P"),
    p("Calculate |BP| and |dBP/dx| with the stated geometric and current data."),
    sub("f.1. Silver magnetic moment"),
    p("For vz=500 m/s and measured separation Δx=0.20 cm, find μs."),
    sub("g.1. Velocity-induced spot spread"),
    p("If beam speed has a 20% spread, estimate the spot-position uncertainty. State how you interpret that percentage."),
    sub("h.1. Magnetic-moment uncertainty"),
    p("Estimate the corresponding error bar on μs."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("The Stern-Gerlach experiment"),
    sub("a.1. Thermal beam speed"),
    p("Equipartition gives mvz²/2=3kBT/2, so vz≈\\(5.26\\times10^{2}\\) m/s."),
    sub("b.1. Beam splitting"),
    p("Transverse acceleration magnitude is μs|Gx|/m. Its deflection during the magnet and subsequent drift is \\(\\mu_s|G_x|[l_2^2/2+l_2l_3]/(mv_z^2)\\). Two opposite moments double this: \\[\\Delta x=\\frac{2\\mu_s|G_x|l_2(l_2/2+l_3)}{mv_z^2}.\\] The pre-magnet distance l1 has no effect."),
    sub("c.1. Two-wire magnetic field"),
    p("Superpose the two wire fields. Let \\(D_\\pm=x^2+(y\\pm a)^2\\). Then \\[\\mathbf B=\\frac{\\mu I_0a}{\\pi D_+D_-}[(x^2-y^2+a^2)\\hat x+2xy\\hat y].\\] This sign matches the stated upper +z and lower −z currents."),
    sub("c.2. Field direction on a circular boundary"),
    p("At R, y=0 and B points +x. At P0=(xc,√(xc²+a²)) the x component vanishes and the field points +y for xc>0. More generally on this right-hand circular arc the field is normal to the circle, as its cross product with (x−xc,y) vanishes."),
    sub("c.3. Air-gap axial field"),
    p("Set y=0 in C1: \\(\\mathbf B=\\mu I_0a\\hat x/[\\pi(x^2+a^2)]\\). The permeability in this model remains μ from the original magnetic circuit."),
    sub("d.1. Dipole force magnitude"),
    p("Differentiate Bx. For x>0 the gradient is negative, \\(dB_x/dx=-2\\mu I_0ax/[\\pi(x^2+a^2)^2]\\). The force magnitude is \\(2\\mu_s\\mu I_0a|x|/[\\pi(x^2+a^2)^2]\\), with opposite signs for opposite moments. The field checks x>0."),
    sub("e.1. Field and gradient at P"),
    p("The right intersections are xR=xc+√(xc²+a²) and xQ=xd+√(xd²+a²). Thus xP=(xR+xQ)/2≈0.01624 m. Substitution gives B≈0.160 T and gradient magnitude ≈17.3 T/m. The signed gradient is negative."),
    sub("f.1. Silver magnetic moment"),
    p("Invert B1: \\(\\mu_s=m\\Delta x\\,v_z^2/[2|G_x|l_2(l_2/2+l_3)]\\). With |Gx|≈17.34 T/m this gives μs≈\\(1.04\\times10^{-23}\\) J/T."),
    sub("g.1. Velocity-induced spot spread"),
    p("Each spot displacement is xspot=Δx/2=0.10 cm and scales as vz⁻². Linear error propagation gives |δxspot|/xspot=2|δvz|/vz. Interpreting 20% as ±20% gives ±0.04 cm, hence positions from 0.06 to 0.14 cm in magnitude. If 20% is the full speed width, use ±10% and half this spot uncertainty. The source accepts both conventions."),
    sub("h.1. Magnetic-moment uncertainty"),
    p("Under the ±20% speed convention the inferred splitting uncertainty is ±40%, so \\(\\mu_s=(1.04\\pm0.42)\\times10^{-23}\\,\\mathrm{J/T}\\). The full interval is approximately 0.62 to \\(1.46\\times10^{-23}\\) J/T. A full-width 20% convention gives half that uncertainty. The source omits the factor \\(10^{-23}\\) in its final line, restored here."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2022-t3"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Maxwell, Rayleigh and Mount Everest"),
    p("Model a neutral air molecule as a fixed point charge +q inside a uniform spherical negative cloud of total charge −q,mass m,radius r. Its small natural vibration frequency is ω0. Light drives E=E0cosωt ŷ and cloud displacement y=y0cosωt. Use the negative charge sign in its force. For low-frequency radiation assume ω≪ω0. Mean optical intensity is I=cε0E0²/2. Molecule number density is n0 and scattered power per molecule is s. For numerical estimates take q=e=\\(1.602\\times10^{-19}\\) C,m=me=\\(9.109\\times10^{-31}\\) kg,ε0=\\(8.854\\times10^{-12}\\) SI,c=\\(3.0\\times10^{8}\\) m/s,n0=\\(2.54\\times10^{25}\\) m⁻³,ω0=\\(1.25\\times10^{16}\\) rad/s and ω=\\(3.25\\times10^{15}\\) rad/s. Darjeeling is h=2042 m above sea level. Everest has height H=8848 m,distance d=170 km, and Kanchenjunga has H=8586 m,d=75 km. Earth radius is R=6378 km. Ignore refraction and variation of molecular density with altitude. For the source's intensity comparison use equal effective source luminosities and geometric dilution I∝d⁻², plus exponential atmospheric attenuation. The unpolluted Kanchenjunga intensity at Darjeeling defines the fixed reference. A peak is visible if its intensity is at least 5% of this reference."),
    sub("a.1. Driven cloud equation"),
    p("Write the equation of motion for y."),
    sub("a.2. Oscillation amplitude"),
    p("Find the forced sinusoidal solution and amplitude."),
    sub("a.3. Induced dipole"),
    p("Find the time-dependent dipole for ω≪ω0."),
    sub("a.4. Natural frequency"),
    p("Derive ω0 from q,m,r."),
    sub("b.1. Radiated-power dimensions"),
    p("Use dimensional analysis to find how mean radiated power depends on p0,ε0,c,ω, up to a dimensionless constant."),
    sub("b.2. Low-frequency radiated power"),
    p("Take the dimensionless constant as 1/(12π). Express s using E0,ω0,ω and the molecular parameters."),
    sub("c.1. Attenuation equation"),
    p("Write the differential equation for I(x)."),
    sub("c.2. Attenuation length"),
    p("Solve with I(0)=I0 and find L."),
    sub("c.3. Molecular attenuation estimate"),
    p("Calculate L in kilometres."),
    sub("d.1. Visible mountain heights"),
    fig("batch8-mountain-curvature", "Original Earth centre, observer height, mountain height and central angular separation."),
    p("Accounting for Earth curvature and an observer unable to see below the local horizontal, derive the apparent vertical height H′ of each peak and evaluate it. Draw the relevant geometry."),
    sub("e.1. Unpolluted visibility ratio"),
    p("Find Everest intensity relative to the unpolluted Kanchenjunga reference and decide if Everest is visible."),
    sub("f.1. Aerosol attenuation"),
    p("Pollution attenuation length depends on particle number density n and cross-section πr², with dimensionless prefactor 1/8. Find Lp and evaluate for aerosol mass density ρp=5 μg/m³,particle radius r=500 nm and material density ρ=3 g/cm³."),
    sub("g.1. Polluted visibility"),
    p("With that uniform pollution, find the intensity of each mountain relative to the original fixed reference. Which peaks remain visible?"),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Maxwell, Rayleigh and Mount Everest"),
    sub("a.1. Driven cloud equation"),
    p("The restoring acceleration is −ω0²y and the electric force is −qE. Hence \\(\\ddot y+\\omega_0^2y=-(qE_0/m)\\cos\\omega t\\)."),
    sub("a.2. Oscillation amplitude"),
    p("Substitute y=y0cosωt to obtain \\(y_0=qE_0/[m(\\omega^2-\\omega_0^2)]\\). This signed coefficient is negative below resonance. The nonnegative amplitude is its absolute value. The steady particular solution excludes free oscillations and resonance."),
    sub("a.3. Induced dipole"),
    p("The dipole vector points from negative to positive charge, so \\(\\mathbf p=-qy\\hat y\\simeq[q^2E_0/(m\\omega_0^2)]\\cos\\omega t\\hat y\\). Its oscillation amplitude is q²E0/(mω0²), and its instantaneous magnitude takes the absolute value of the cosine. This distinguishes the signed sinusoid from the nonnegative magnitude."),
    sub("a.4. Natural frequency"),
    p("Inside the uniform cloud the field grows linearly with displacement. Action and reaction give restoring force \\(-q^2y/(4\\pi\\epsilon_0r^3)\\). Therefore \\(\\omega_0=q/\\sqrt{4\\pi\\epsilon_0mr^3}\\)."),
    sub("b.1. Radiated-power dimensions"),
    p("Matching dimensions in s=kp0αωβε0γcδ gives α=2,β=4,γ=−1,δ=−3. Thus \\(s=k p_0^2\\omega^4/(\\epsilon_0c^3)\\)."),
    sub("b.2. Low-frequency radiated power"),
    p("Use p0=q²E0/(mω0²): \\[s=\\frac{q^4E_0^2}{12\\pi\\epsilon_0m^2c^3}\\left(\\frac\\omega{\\omega_0}\\right)^4.\\]"),
    sub("c.1. Attenuation equation"),
    p("Balance power in a slab of area A and thickness dx: I(x)A−I(x+dx)A=n0sA dx. Hence \\(dI/dx=-n_0s\\). Since s∝E0²∝I this is dI/dx=−I/L."),
    sub("c.2. Attenuation length"),
    p("Combine B2 with I=cε0E0²/2 to obtain \\[L=\\frac{6\\pi\\epsilon_0^2m^2c^4}{n_0q^4}\\left(\\frac{\\omega_0}{\\omega}\\right)^4,\\qquad I(x)=I_0e^{-x/L}.\\]"),
    sub("c.3. Molecular attenuation estimate"),
    p("Substitution of the supplied molecular and optical data gives approximately L=130 km. The source uses rounded constants and a ten-percent numerical acceptance range."),
    sub("d.1. Visible mountain heights"),
    p("Let θ=d/R. In the source projection construction the local horizontal through the observer intersects the mountain radius at radius (R+h)secθ. Similar triangles give \\[H^{\\prime}=R+h-\\frac{(R+h)^2}{R+H}\\sec(d/R).\\] To leading order in h/R,H/R,d/R, \\(H^{\\prime}\\simeq H-h-d^2/(2R)\\). The full source construction gives approximately 6096 m for Kanchenjunga and 4534 m for Everest. Both extend above the local horizontal. Show Earth centre, observer radius, mountain radius and the observer's tangent line."),
    sub("e.1. Unpolluted visibility ratio"),
    p("For equal effective luminosities, \\[\\frac{I_E}{I_K}=\\left(\\frac{75}{170}\\right)^2e^{-(170-75)/130}\\simeq0.093.\\] The distances and L must use the same units. This exceeds 0.05, so Everest is visible in this model."),
    sub("f.1. Aerosol attenuation"),
    p("\\(L_p=1/(8n\\pi r^2)\\). Each particle has mass 4πr³ρ/3, so n=ρp/(4πr³ρ/3), giving \\(L_p=r\\rho/(6\\rho_p)\\). Convert ρp=\\(5\\times10^{-9}\\) kg/m³ and ρ=3000 kg/m³ to obtain Lp=50 km."),
    sub("g.1. Polluted visibility"),
    p("The total attenuation has exponent −d/L−d/Lp. Thus \\(I_K/I_{ref}=e^{-75/50}\\simeq0.223\\), while \\(I_E/I_{ref}=0.093e^{-170/50}\\simeq0.0031\\). Kanchenjunga remains above 5%, while Everest does not. The reference remains the unpolluted Kanchenjunga intensity, rather than being reset after pollution."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2025-t2"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Waves and phase transitions in spin systems"),
    p("Treat spin vectors classically except for E=ℏω,p=ℏk in the quasiparticle interpretation. A rotating uniform ring has positive charge Q,mass M,radius R and magnetic moment μ=γL. In A3 the separation d≫R is perpendicular to the ring moments as in the diagram. In B a long chain of fixed spin vectors of magnitude S lies along x at spacing a, with energy \\(E=-J\\sum_i\\mathbf S_i\\cdot\\mathbf S_{i+1}\\), J>0. Only nearest neighbours interact. For weak waves use Siz≈S and Ṡiz≈0. In C each spin is restricted to si=±1 along z and \\(E=-\\tilde J\\sum_i s_is_{i+1}-h\\sum_i s_i\\), with N≫1, J̃≥0 and temperature T. Ignore edges. The source's prescribed one-neighbour energy-replacement approximation in C4-C7 sets J̃eff=J̃s̄. This exercise approximation differs from the usual two-neighbour local-field mean-field model. Neither approximation is the exact thermodynamics of a one-dimensional short-range Ising chain, which has no nonzero-temperature ordered phase."),
    fig("batch8-larmor-ring", "Original charged ring angular momentum inclined to an external magnetic field."),
    sub("a.1. Ring gyromagnetic ratio"),
    p("Find γ relating μ to L."),
    sub("a.2. Signed Larmor frequency"),
    p("Place the ring in B ẑ. Find ωL, with positive precession counterclockwise viewed from +z."),
    sub("a.3. Dipole coupling energy"),
    fig("batch8-spin-ring-coupling", "Original two transverse rotating rings separated by d with relative moment angle theta."),
    p("Two ring moments are transverse to their separation and make angle θ to one another. Find J0 in U=J0L1·L2."),
    sub("b.1. Exchange effective field"),
    p("Find Bi,eff acting on spin i."),
    sub("b.2. Spin evolution"),
    p("Find dSi/dt."),
    sub("b.3. Spin-wave dispersion"),
    fig("batch8-spin-wave", "Original small transverse spin oscillations about the magnetized chain."),
    p("Find ω(k) using a travelling transverse wave."),
    sub("b.4. Magnon effective mass"),
    p("For |ka|≪1 find meff."),
    sub("b.5. Neutron-scattering mass"),
    fig("batch8-neutron-scattering", "Original incident and outgoing neutron angles measured from the chain normal."),
    p("A low-energy neutron scatters in xy and excites one long-wavelength spin wave while the chain stays at rest. Find meff using neutron mass mn and θin,θout. Follow the diagram: these angles are measured from the y normal, despite the source prose saying with the chain."),
    sub("c.1. Independent-spin probability ratio"),
    p("For J̃=0 find p↑/p↓ in terms of signed h,T,kB."),
    sub("c.2. Polarization curve"),
    p("Find s̄ for J̃=0 and sketch it on −h0≤h≤h0 for h0≫kBT,h0≈kBT and h0≪kBT."),
    sub("c.3. Ground-state energy"),
    p("Now h=0,J̃>0. Find Eg."),
    sub("c.4. Prescribed average-energy replacement"),
    p("In the exercise approximation replace si+1 by s̄ in the bond sum and write E=−J̃effΣsi. Find J̃eff."),
    sub("c.5. Self-consistency and critical temperature"),
    p("Using C2 and C4 derive the equation for s̄ and find Tc where the number of solutions changes."),
    sub("c.6. Near-critical branches"),
    p("For 0<Tc−T≪Tc find all roots and sketch the solution branches for 0≤T≤2Tc."),
    sub("c.7. Magnetic phases in the approximation"),
    p("Classify T>Tc and T<Tc as paramagnetic or ferromagnetic."),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Waves and phase transitions in spin systems"),
    sub("a.1. Ring gyromagnetic ratio"),
    p("\\(L=MR^2\\omega\\) and \\(\\mu=Q\\omega R^2/2\\), hence \\(\\gamma=Q/(2M)\\)."),
    sub("a.2. Signed Larmor frequency"),
    p("Torque is \\(\\dot{\\mathbf L}=\\gamma\\mathbf L\\times\\mathbf B\\). Compare with \\(\\dot{\\mathbf L}=\\omega_L\\hat z\\times\\mathbf L\\), giving \\(\\omega_L=-\\gamma B\\)."),
    sub("a.3. Dipole coupling energy"),
    p("The equatorial field of ring 1 is \\(\\mathbf B_1=-\\mu_0\\boldsymbol\\mu_1/(4\\pi d^3)\\). Thus U=−μ2·B1 gives \\(J_0=\\mu_0\\gamma^2/(4\\pi d^3)\\), positive."),
    sub("b.1. Exchange effective field"),
    p("The terms containing Si are \\(-J\\mathbf S_i\\cdot(\\mathbf S_{i-1}+\\mathbf S_{i+1})\\). Comparing with −γSi·Bi,eff gives \\(\\mathbf B_{i,eff}=(J/\\gamma)(\\mathbf S_{i-1}+\\mathbf S_{i+1})\\)."),
    sub("b.2. Spin evolution"),
    p("Apply magnetic torque: \\[\\frac{d\\mathbf S_i}{dt}=J\\mathbf S_i\\times(\\mathbf S_{i-1}+\\mathbf S_{i+1}).\\] Each spin keeps constant magnitude because its derivative is perpendicular to itself."),
    sub("b.3. Spin-wave dispersion"),
    p("Linearization gives \\(\\dot S_{ix}=JS(2S_{iy}-S_{i-1,y}-S_{i+1,y})\\) and \\(\\dot S_{iy}=-JS(2S_{ix}-S_{i-1,x}-S_{i+1,x})\\). Take equal-amplitude quadrature waves Sx=δScos(kai−ωt), Sy=δSsin(kai−ωt). Then \\[\\omega=2JS[1-\\cos(ka)].\\] The opposite phase convention can reverse signed frequency. The field checks its positive branch."),
    sub("b.4. Magnon effective mass"),
    p("\\(\\omega\\simeq JSa^2k^2\\). With E=ℏω,p=ℏk, compare E=(JSa²/ℏ)p² with p²/(2meff), giving \\(m_{eff}=\\hbar/(2JSa^2)\\)."),
    sub("b.5. Neutron-scattering mass"),
    p("The spin wave has no y momentum, so pin cosθin=pout cosθout. Its x momentum is ps=pin sinθin−pout sinθout and energy Es=(pin²−pout²)/(2mn). Thus \\[m_{eff}=m_n\\frac{\\sin^2(\\theta_{in}-\\theta_{out})}{\\cos^2\\theta_{out}-\\cos^2\\theta_{in}}=m_n\\frac{\\sin(\\theta_{in}-\\theta_{out})}{\\sin(\\theta_{in}+\\theta_{out})}.\\] Choose physical angles giving positive energy loss and mass. If angles are instead measured from the chain, convert them before using this expression."),
    sub("c.1. Independent-spin probability ratio"),
    p("The ±z states have energies −h and +h. Boltzmann weights give \\(p_\\uparrow/p_\\downarrow=e^{2h/(k_BT)}\\). For negative h the labels remain ±z, so up is then anti-aligned with the field."),
    sub("c.2. Polarization curve"),
    p("Normalize the two Boltzmann weights to obtain \\(\\bar s=p_\\uparrow-p_\\downarrow=\\tanh[h/(k_BT)]\\). All curves are odd through the origin. For a large field range they saturate near ±1, for a small range they are nearly linear with slope 1/(kBT), and the intermediate range gives a smooth unsaturated S-shaped curve."),
    sub("c.3. Ground-state energy"),
    p("All spins align, with either global sign, so every bond contributes −J̃. For an open chain Eg=−J̃(N−1)≈−J̃N. A periodic chain has exactly N bonds."),
    sub("c.4. Prescribed average-energy replacement"),
    p("The explicitly prescribed replacement gives \\(\\tilde J_{eff}=\\tilde J\\bar s\\). This matches the official scheme's single-counted energy ansatz. For comparison, a thermodynamically consistent local-field decoupling uses both neighbours, field 2J̃s̄ and an additive correction NJ̃s̄². Those are different approximations, so the next items use the one requested here."),
    sub("c.5. Self-consistency and critical temperature"),
    p("For the source ansatz, \\(\\bar s=\\tanh[\\tilde J\\bar s/(k_BT)]\\). The zero solution always exists. Nonzero roots appear when the right-hand slope at zero exceeds one, giving \\(T_c=\\tilde J/k_B\\). Above Tc only zero exists, below Tc there are also two opposite roots. This is the transition of the stated approximation, not an exact 1D transition."),
    sub("c.6. Near-critical branches"),
    p("Expand tanh x≈x−x³/3. The roots are \\(\\bar s=0\\) and \\(\\bar s\\simeq\\pm\\sqrt{3(T_c-T)/T_c}\\). Keeping cubic-order temperature factors gives ±√[3(T/Tc)²(1−T/Tc)]. Draw the full roots from the implicit equation, not the near-critical expansion down to T=0: nonzero branches approach ±1 with zero slope at zero temperature and merge with vertical tangents at Tc. The zero branch persists below Tc but is unstable in the mean-field picture."),
    sub("c.7. Magnetic phases in the approximation"),
    p("The approximate model is paramagnetic above Tc and ferromagnetic below Tc, where it supports spontaneous nonzero s̄. The exact infinite one-dimensional short-range chain orders only at zero temperature."),
  ],
};

nativeOlympiadContent["apho-2025-t3"] = {
  statement: [
    h("Atmospheric physics"),
    p("Use ideal gases with five molecular degrees of freedom for air, so molar cp=7R/2. Constants: solar irradiance Fs=1370 W/m², albedo a=0.30, σ=\\(5.67\\times10^{-8}\\) W m⁻² K⁻⁴, molar masses μw=0.018 kg/mol and μair=0.029 kg/mol, R=8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. Earth radiates as a blackbody. In the one-layer model use shortwave transmission tsw and longwave transmission tlw, with the remaining absorbed power re-radiated equally upwards and downwards as prescribed by the exercise. All atmospheric parcels stay in pressure equilibrium with their surroundings. Gravity g is uniform and downward. Define lapse rate Γ=−dT/dz, positive when air cools with height. Latent heat L is per mass and may be treated constant over the temperature interval. In E use air index one and ice index n=1.31."),
    fig("batch8-atmospheric-layer", "Original isothermal ground and thin atmospheric layer."),
    sub("a.1. Absorbed solar power"),
    p("Find total net solar power P0 received by Earth plus atmosphere using Earth radius RE."),
    sub("a.2. Airless temperature"),
    p("Ignoring the atmosphere find steady ground temperature Tg0."),
    sub("a.3. One-layer greenhouse temperature"),
    p("For tsw=0.9 and tlw=0.2 calculate Tg."),
    sub("b.1. Diatomic vibrational frequency"),
    p("Two atoms mA,mB are connected by a spring of stiffness k. Find their vibrational angular frequency."),
    sub("b.2. Vibrational photon"),
    p("Find photon energy for a transition between adjacent vibrational levels, neglecting recoil."),
    sub("b.3. Doppler-shift convention"),
    p("A molecule moves with speed v>0 towards the emitter. Find the first-order line shift. Distinguish the frequency it sees for fixed incident lab light from the lab frequency resonant with its transition f0."),
    sub("b.4. Velocity normalization"),
    p("Normalize p1(v)=Cexp[−mv²/(2kBT)] over the real line."),
    sub("b.5. Thermal spectral profile"),
    p("Find p2(f), up to normalization, for a rest line f0 broadened by one-dimensional thermal motion."),
    sub("b.6. Doppler width and sketch"),
    p("Sketch p2 against f−f0 and find f*−f0 at 1/e of the peak."),
    sub("c.1. Hydrostatic pressure gradient"),
    fig("batch8-air-parcel", "Original air parcel in a hydrostatic pressure and density profile."),
    p("Derive dp/dz using density ρ and g."),
    sub("c.2. Ideal-gas gradient"),
    p("Express dp/dz through μair,g,p,T and R."),
    sub("c.3. Isothermal barometric law"),
    p("For constant T and surface pressure p0 find p(z)."),
    sub("c.4. Dry adiabatic lapse rate"),
    p("Find Γa for a rising adiabatic parcel using molar cp."),
    sub("c.5. Buoyancy stability"),
    p("An initially equilibrated parcel is displaced by δz in atmosphere of lapse Γ. Derive its linear equation, stability condition and angular frequency."),
    sub("d.1. Clausius-Clapeyron slope"),
    p("Find dps/dT for liquid-vapour coexistence using L,μw,ps,T and R. Neglect liquid volume relative to vapour."),
    sub("d.2. Saturation-pressure law"),
    p("At T0 let ps=ps0. Find ps(T) assuming constant L."),
    sub("d.3. Cloud formation temperature"),
    p("A moist parcel starts at Ti=17.0°C,pi=\\(10^{5}\\) Pa with fixed water mass fraction φ=0.010. Find Tl where condensation begins. Use L=\\(2.460\\times10^{6}\\) J/kg and ps0=1940 Pa at Ti. Treat cp=7R/2 and use the dilute-water approximation."),
    sub("e.1. Prism deviation law"),
    fig("batch8-halo-prism", "Original solar halo photograph and ray deviations through a triangular prism."),
    p("For prism apex angle φ, refractive index n and incident angle α from the face normal, derive outgoing deviation δ."),
    sub("e.2. Hexagonal-ice deviation plot"),
    fig("batch8-ice-hexagon", "Original ray path through two faces of a hexagonal ice prism equivalent to a sixty-degree apex."),
    p("The ray traverses the two rectangular faces indicated in the source hexagon, equivalent to φ=60°. For n=1.31 tabulate and plot δ from α=20° to 70° in 5° increments."),
    sub("e.3. Bright halo angle"),
    p("At what angular distance from the Sun is the halo brightest?"),
  ],
  solution: [
    h("Atmospheric physics"),
    sub("a.1. Absorbed solar power"),
    p("Use the intercepting disc and the absorbed fraction: \\(P_0=(1-a)\\pi R_E^2F_s\\)."),
    sub("a.2. Airless temperature"),
    p("Equate absorbed disc power to emission over 4πRE²: \\(T_{g0}=[(1-a)F_s/(4\\sigma)]^{1/4}\\simeq255\\,\\mathrm K\\), about −18°C."),
    sub("a.3. One-layer greenhouse temperature"),
    p("Let PE be ground emission and PA the emission of each atmospheric face. Surface balance gives PE=PA+tswP0. Top-of-atmosphere balance gives tlwPE+PA=P0. Hence \\(T_g=[(1+t_{sw})/(1+t_{lw})]^{1/4}T_{g0}\\simeq286\\,\\mathrm K\\), about 13°C."),
    sub("b.1. Diatomic vibrational frequency"),
    p("The relative coordinate has reduced mass μ=mAmB/(mA+mB). Thus \\(\\omega_d=\\sqrt{k(m_A+m_B)/(m_Am_B)}\\)."),
    sub("b.2. Vibrational photon"),
    p("Harmonic-oscillator adjacent levels differ by \\(E_p=\\hbar\\omega_d\\)."),
    sub("b.3. Doppler-shift convention"),
    p("For fixed incoming lab frequency fin, the molecule sees f′≈fin(1+v/c). Thus if fin=f0 the observed shift is +f0v/c, the convention used in the official solution. If f0 instead denotes the molecule's rest-frame absorption resonance, the resonant laboratory incident frequency is \\(f=f_0/(1+v/c)\\simeq f_0-f_0v/c\\), with shift −f0v/c. The source wording mixes these descriptions. Both yield the same symmetric thermal line profile below, so this signed convention item uses self-review."),
    sub("b.4. Velocity normalization"),
    p("The Gaussian integral is √(2πkBT/m), so \\(C=\\sqrt{m/(2\\pi k_BT)}\\)."),
    sub("b.5. Thermal spectral profile"),
    p("Use |v|=c|f−f0|/f0 and the constant Jacobian c/f0. Thus \\[p_2(f)\\propto\\exp\\left[-\\frac{mc^2(f-f_0)^2}{2k_BT f_0^2}\\right].\\] If normalized, the prefactor is (c/f0)√[m/(2πkBT)]."),
    sub("b.6. Doppler width and sketch"),
    p("The curve is a symmetric Gaussian centred at zero, decaying to zero in both tails. The 1/e points are \\(f_*-f_0=\\pm f_0\\sqrt{2k_BT/(mc^2)}\\). The field checks the positive half-width."),
    sub("c.1. Hydrostatic pressure gradient"),
    p("Balance upward minus downward pressure forces on area A and height dz against weight ρgA dz. This gives \\(dp/dz=-\\rho g\\)."),
    sub("c.2. Ideal-gas gradient"),
    p("Ideal gas gives ρ=μairp/(RT), hence \\(dp/dz=-\\mu_{air}gp/(RT)\\)."),
    sub("c.3. Isothermal barometric law"),
    p("Integrate dp/p=−μairg dz/(RT) to obtain \\(p(z)=p_0e^{-\\mu_{air}gz/(RT)}\\)."),
    sub("c.4. Dry adiabatic lapse rate"),
    p("Adiabatic enthalpy change balances gravitational potential: cp dT+μairg dz=0. Equivalently combine pVγ=constant with hydrostatic equilibrium. Thus \\(\\Gamma_a=\\mu_{air}g/c_p\\)."),
    sub("c.5. Buoyancy stability"),
    p("Parcel and environment temperatures become T−Γaδz and T−Γδz. At equal pressure ρ∝1/T, so net buoyancy acceleration is \\[\\delta\\ddot z=\\frac gT(\\Gamma-\\Gamma_a)\\delta z.\\] Stability requires Γ<Γa, with \\(\\omega=\\sqrt{g(\\mu_{air}g/c_p-\\Gamma)/T}\\). Equality is neutral, while Γ>Γa gives exponential growth. This sign follows the defined positive lapse rate."),
    sub("d.1. Clausius-Clapeyron slope"),
    p("For evaporated mass m, ΔS=Lm/T while ΔV≈(m/μw)RT/ps. Thus \\(d p_s/dT=\\mu_w Lp_s/(RT^2)\\)."),
    sub("d.2. Saturation-pressure law"),
    p("Integration of dps/ps=(μwL/R)dT/T² gives \\[p_s(T)=p_{s0}\\exp\\left[-\\frac{\\mu_wL}{R}(1/T-1/T_0)\\right].\\]"),
    sub("d.3. Cloud formation temperature"),
    p("The water partial pressure is \\(p_w\\simeq\\phi(\\mu_{air}/\\mu_w)p\\), and dry adiabatic ascent gives p=pi(T/Ti)cp/R. Solve \\[\\phi\\frac{\\mu_{air}}{\\mu_w}p_i(T_l/T_i)^{7/2}=p_{s0}\\exp\\left[-\\frac{\\mu_wL}{R}(1/T_l-1/T_i)\\right]\\] for the first lower-temperature crossing. With Ti=290.15 K the root is Tl≈286.8 K, or 13.7°C. The total-mass definition of φ changes this only slightly beyond the dilute approximation."),
    sub("e.1. Prism deviation law"),
    p("Snell gives internal entry α′=arcsin(sinα/n) and internal exit β′=φ−α′. The external exit angle is β=arcsin(nsinβ′). Therefore \\[\\delta=\\alpha+\\arcsin\\{n\\sin[\\varphi-\\arcsin(\\sin\\alpha/n)]\\}-\\varphi.\\] A transmitted ray requires the exit sine magnitude no greater than one."),
    sub("e.2. Hexagonal-ice deviation plot"),
    p("The (α,δ) pairs in degrees are approximately (20,27.5),(25,24.6),(30,23.0),(35,22.2),(40,21.8),(45,22.0),(50,22.5),(55,23.4),(60,24.7),(65,26.5),(70,28.7). Draw a smooth curve with a minimum close to α=41°. Label both axes in degrees."),
    sub("e.3. Bright halo angle"),
    p("The random crystal orientations concentrate rays near minimum deviation. Symmetric passage gives \\(\\delta_{min}=2\\arcsin[n\\sin(\\varphi/2)]-\\varphi\\simeq21.8^\\circ\\), the familiar approximately 22° halo."),
  ],
};
